dsh: reasoning:
Important: Plan says "german-ats keeps its look, but its DOM order becomes main column then sidebar, with CSS order for the visual position." But in the current code, the sidebar comes first in DOM (aside at line 231) then main (line 368). Wait, plan says DOM order becomes main column then sidebar - currently sidebar first. Hmm - the review said "german-ats DOM order puts the sidebar before experience, so ATS reading order breaks". Plan's fix: main then sidebar in DOM with CSS order. But if you put main first in DOM and use CSS `order` for visual position, does Browser Run's Chromium text extraction follow DOM order or visual (paint) order? PDF text extraction order in Chromium/Skia is generally based on drawing order, which follows CSS order/paint order, not DOM. Actually Skia emits text in the order it's drawn, which follows paint order. With `order`, the visual layout is reordered, and text is painted in visual order. So the fix may not work: CSS `order` reorders layout and paint. To get main-first reading order in PDF, you'd need main visually first or absolute positioning of sidebar while keeping DOM order. This is a real finding - and section 6 says "tagged PDF" too. Tagged PDF structure tree order would follow DOM order (if tagging works), but text extraction order from untagged PDF follows paint order. This is a key adversarial finding. Let me verify: In Chromium print-to-PDF, text is drawn in paint order of the layout tree. Flex `order` affects layout order (and thus paint order). So DOM order main-then-sidebar plus `order` placing sidebar first visually would still emit sidebar text first in the content stream. Therefore plan's claimed fix does not actually fix the extraction order. Unless they keep sidebar visually left but draw text after... impossible with flex order. Could use `position: absolute` for sidebar so it's painted after? Actually absolutely positioned elements are painted in a later stacking layer, order in tree. Hmm. Regardless, the claim needs a spike test with pdftotext order, which plan's spike 0b doesn't include (only fonts/photo/tagging). The eval does check reading order via pdftotext. So it'd be caught at eval time, but late. Worth flagging.

Also check plan: "tagged is on" - does Cloudflare Browser Run quickAction pdf support `tagged` in pdfOptions? Need to verify docs. Let me check what pdfOptions supports. I recall Cloudflare Browser Rendering `/pdf` endpoint options: format, landscape, printBackground, scale, margin, preferCSSPageSize, displayHeaderFooter, headerTemplate, footerTemplate, pageRanges, width, height, timeout, omitBackground, tagged, outline... Actually Cloudflare's PDF endpoint supports `tagged` and `outline` as of some version. Let me research current Cloudflare docs. Also `quickAction` - is that the API? Cloudflare Browser Rendering has `env.BROWSER.quickAction('pdf', {...})` in new API? I recall in 2025 Cloudflare introduced "Browser Rendering quick actions" via binding: `env.BROWSER.quickAction("screenshot", {...})` and `"pdf"`. Let me verify from docs via web search. Also font support: Browser Run doesn't allow custom fonts? Actually there's a restriction: `/pdf` endpoint doesn't support web fonts? Hmm. Known Cloudflare Browser Rendering limitations: "Custom fonts are not supported in /pdf endpoint" maybe. Let me recall: Cloudflare Browser Rendering docs for PDF: "The /pdf endpoint does not support ... " I remember a note: "Page breaks inside tables are not supported"? Let's web search. Also `tagged` support may not exist. Actually Chrome DevTools Protocol Page.printToPDF has `generateTaggedPDF` (experimental). Cloudflare's REST API may expose `tagged`. Need to check.

