No `.cv-template` CSS at all. So the canvas relies mostly on inline styles; Tailwind classes like `bg-white shadow-lg mx-auto print:shadow-none`, `print:hidden`, `hidden print:block`. For server render, only a few utility classes matter, plus `print:shadow-none` and `print:hidden`. The plan says "print mode that hides edit buttons". The templates have `print:hidden` for score badges and a `hidden print:block` footer page number? Let's see line 686. That's a footer with page number? It says "hidden print:block fixed bottom-4 right-8 text-xs text-gray-400" maybe a "Made with Useful Apply" footer. For server render, they'd need Tailwind CSS for these; they could replace with inline styles in print mode. Not a huge blocker, but the plan's "same components" claim needs a print-mode prop; it says that. Fine.

**oklch colors**: The current browser export strips oklch because html2canvas can't parse. With Chromium, oklch is supported (Chrome 111+). The plan doesn't mention oklch at all, but it doesn't need to. However the templates use Tailwind classes `bg-white` etc. which compile to oklch in Tailwind v4 (colors in oklch). Chromium handles it. Fine. The task explicitly asks "oklch colours" - I can dismiss: Chromium supports oklch, and Browser Run's Chromium is current; no issue. But if they reuse the existing `cv-pdf` browser fallback, it strips oklch -> wrong colors. The plan keeps browser export as a fallback "with a visible notice". The fallback still has oklch stripping and raster text. If the web app falls back to the browser raster, the user gets a low-quality PDF that fails ATS. The plan says "If Browser Run fails, the browser export runs as a fallback, with a visible notice and a PostHog event. MCP returns an error rather than a degraded file." That's a quality inconsistency: the web app would still ship a non-ATS PDF (image) on fallback, violating "best-in-class ATS" and "same renderer". Better: fail with a retry, or fallback only for preview. This is a real finding: the fallback undermines the export quality guarantee and is not "the same renderer". Severity high/medium. The plan explicitly says no degraded file for MCP but allows one for humans. The product goal says web app must use the same renderer and exports must be best-in-class. A visible notice doesn't fix an image-only PDF that ATS can't parse. Add finding (high).

Also: **Browser Run availability** - Browser Run is not available in local dev, spike uses `wrangler dev --remote`. Fine. But production `npm run deploy:cf` needs a Browser binding added to wrangler. Plan's PR 3 adds. Fine.

**Browser Run cost**: Plan says 10 hours/month included, then $0.09/hr, 3s/PDF = $0.000075/PDF. 3s/3600 * $0.09 = $0.000075. Correct. At 12,000 PDFs: 10 hours = 36,000s / 3 = 12,000. Correct. But Browser Run bills browser time with a minimum? Cloudflare Browser Rendering pricing: free 10 min/day? Workers Paid includes 10 hours/month? Let me check. As of 2025, Browser Rendering: Workers Paid includes 10 hours per month, then $0.09/hour; free plan 10 minutes/day. Plan says Workers Paid 10 hours a month. Likely right. But **concurrency**: Browser Rendering has a limit of 10 concurrent browsers (or 20?) and per-account. `quickAction` requests queue; a burst of exports could hit concurrency limits and time out. Plan doesn't mention. Low.

Now **Queue limits**: Cloudflare Queues has a max message size 128 KB, batch size etc. Message carries only operation id, fine. Consumer wall time 15 min. Fine. But **Queues requires a paid plan**? Workers Paid, yes. No issue.

**CPU time**: `cpu_ms: 300000` (5 min) is configured. Queue consumer CPU shares? Fine. But Browser Run + PDF + pdf-lib + DOCX generation inside a single request: `export_documents` generates 5 formats in one tool call; if it renders 5 PDFs sequentially with Browser Run at 3s each plus DOCX, total 15-20s wall, within limits. But `quickAction` default `PDFOptions.timeout` 30s; action timeout 5 min. Fine. However: **the export tool `export_documents` with all five formats in two templates in the E2E** could hit Browser Run concurrency and Worker wall time. Not critical.

**PDF-lib metadata**: The plan says `pdf-lib` sets Title, Author, Subject, Language and Producer. pdf-lib can set Title, Author, Subject, Keywords, Creator, Producer, CreationDate, ModDate. It can set Language? PDF catalog `/Lang` is not exposed by pdf-lib's `PDFDocument.setLanguage`? Actually pdf-lib has `setLanguage(lang)` added? I think `PDFDocument` has `setTitle`, `setAuthor`, `setSubject`, `setKeywords`, `setCreator`, `setProducer`, `setCreationDate`, `setModificationDate`, and `setLanguage` maybe not. Let me check pdf-lib API. I recall pdf-lib `PDFDocument` metadata methods: `setTitle`, `setAuthor`, `setSubject`, `setKeywords`, `setCreator`, `setProducer`, `setCreationDate`, `setModificationDate`. There is no `setLanguage`. To set `/Lang`, you'd need low-level `doc.catalog.set(PDFName.of('Lang'), PDFString.of('de-DE'))`. The plan says "pdf-lib then sets Title, Author, Subject, Language and Producer" — if pdf-lib lacks setLanguage, the implementation would need a low-level hack. This is a factual claim to verify. Let me check the installed pdf-lib version/API.
dsh: reasoning:
pdf-lib 1.17.1 has setLanguage. Good, dismiss.

Metadata: "Author" from user name, "Subject" etc. Fine.

**Tagged PDF and pdf-lib**: If the plan runs the Browser Run PDF through pdf-lib to set metadata and save, does that preserve tagged structure and fonts? `PDFDocument.load()` then `save()` preserves objects and structure tree generally, but it may not preserve incremental structure? It should preserve `/StructTreeRoot`. But it can break tagged PDF if it rewrites? pdf-lib loads and saves all objects, so likely preserves. But adding metadata with pdf-lib may drop `/Lang`? No. Still, the spike should test tagging after pdf-lib. Plan says spike 0b tests tagging, then "pdf-lib then sets metadata" — the order is not explicit: render PDF (tagged), then pdf-lib load/save. Spike must test the post-pdf-lib output, not the raw Browser Run output. Minor.

**Text order in two-column layouts**: As noted, plan's fix (DOM main first, CSS order) likely doesn't change PDF text extraction order. Let me verify with Chromium behavior. This is a key finding. Is PDF text extraction order based on content stream order? Yes. Chromium's print backend (Skia) draws text in paint order. A flex item with `order: -1` is laid out and painted before the other. So the sidebar text still comes first. Thus the plan's fix fails. The correct fix: make the sidebar visually first via absolute positioning while keeping DOM order (absolutely positioned elements paint after in-flow content, so text order would be main then sidebar), or accept a single-column ATS template and recommend it, or generate an ATS-specific variant for export (single column) while the canvas keeps two columns — but the plan insists canvas and PDF stay identical. Or use CSS `direction`? No.

Wait: if main is first in DOM and sidebar is second, and you want sidebar visually left, you could use `float: left` on the sidebar? Floats are painted in DOM order but layout positions; text order still main first, sidebar second, but visually sidebar left. In flex, `order` reorders layout *and* paint. Using `float: right` on the sidebar? Main would wrap. Better: grid with explicit grid-column placement (DOM main first, sidebar second, `grid-column: 1`) - does grid paint order follow DOM or grid placement? CSS Grid painting follows order-modified document order, not visual grid position. So grid placement would keep paint/DOM order main first while visually sidebar left. That's the correct technique. So the plan should say "grid placement, not `order`" — `order` changes paint order. This is a precise, high-value finding. Let me verify CSS spec: `order` property affects "order-modified document order" used for painting. Yes. Grid auto-placement vs explicit grid-area: painting order is order-modified document order (DOM), so explicit placement doesn't reorder paint. Good. So fix: keep DOM main first, place sidebar in grid column 1 with `grid-column`, no `order`. Or absolute-position sidebar.

Also, Chromium's tagged PDF structure tree follows DOM order, not visual, so main-first DOM is right for tagging. But text extraction follows content stream. Exactly.

Now **ATS rules correctness/completeness**:
- Section 6 says "Every PDF: Real text with Unicode maps." But **the cover letter PDF** currently is jsPDF Helvetica; plan changes. Fine.
- "No text in images" — icons in CV templates are lucide SVG, rendered as vector paths, not text. Fine.
- **Contact details in body, never header/footer** — good.
- **Standard headings**: German headings from `data.sectionHeaders` (AI output) may be arbitrary; plan says standard headings. But the templates use `data.sectionHeaders?.summary || DEFAULT_HEADERS.summary`; AI output can produce "PROFESSIONAL SUMMARY" even in German if prompt fails. The plan doesn't say to force/normalize headings for the market. The ATS eval checks headings. Fine.
- **US date format**: "Mar 2022 - Present", German "03/2022 - heute". German CVs typically use "MM/YYYY" or "Monat YYYY"; both fine. But `GermanATSTemplate` uses `exp.startDate} – {exp.current ? ...`; the `startDate` may already be "Jan 2020". The plan doesn't say how to normalize dates. Low.
- **Germany: no photo?** Plan says optional, per template. But German ATS norms increasingly recommend no photo; plan says optional. Fine.
- **Personal data**: "Never date of birth, nationality or marital status" for US; "Only fields the user explicitly chose to show" for DACH. But the *existing* GermanATSTemplate renders `dateOfBirth` and `nationality` whenever present (lines 155-174), and there's no "explicitly chose to show" flag. The schema/profile has `date_of_birth` and `nationality`, and the template shows them automatically. Plan says "Only fields the user explicitly chose to show" but doesn't add a visibility flag or tool to control it. This is a concrete gap: the current template exposes DOB/nationality by default; the plan's rule is unimplemented. Also, for US templates, do any show DOB/nationality? Check.
dsh: reasoning:
There is `types.ts` with forbidden/optional fields per template. Let me read around 200-260.
dsh: reasoning:
This is in the repo: COMPLIANCE_CHECKLIST.US.forbidden includes photo/dateOfBirth/maritalStatus/citizenship/streetAddress. But the USPremiumATSTemplate supports a photo (`photoUrl`), and the prior review says "premium-ats supports a photo today". The plan says "Per template, as today. ... No silent change". Fine. But the plan's US table says "Personal data: Never date of birth, nationality or marital status". Current US templates don't render DOB/nationality. Fine. For Germany, the checklist says photo recommended and DOB/nationality optional. The plan says "Only fields the user explicitly chose to show" - but there is no explicit choice. The template shows them whenever present. So the plan's promise is false unless they add a visibility mechanism. This is a good finding. Also, `build-cv-data.ts` copies profile DOB/nationality into every German CV automatically (lines 131-132), so the user doesn't explicitly choose per application. Add.

Also the German compliance checklist says "signature required at bottom with city and date" and "2cm all sides" and "dateFormat DD.MM.YYYY". The plan says A4 25mm left/20mm right (differs from the repo's 2cm all sides) and doesn't mention the German CV signature requirement. The plan says cover letter only for DIN. But a German CV traditionally includes a signature line (the template has `data.signature.includeSignature`), and the plan's section 6 doesn't mention it. Not necessarily required by ATS. But "Germany/EU best-in-class" should address. Low/medium.

Also the plan says DACH templates are `german-ats`, `german-classic`, and "Other EU countries such as UK, Ireland, Netherlands, Nordics use US conventions on A4, via paper override." But the catalog marks `german-ats`/`german-classic` as "DACH and NL". So the plan says NL should use US conventions but the catalog says German templates are best for NL. Contradiction / drift. Low.

Also: **US CV paper**. The web templates are 8.5in x 11in. The plan says US Letter, 0.5-0.75in margins. Premium uses padding 0.5in top/bottom, 0.75in left/right. Fine. But `preferCSSPageSize` plus `@page` margins: if the template root has padding 0.5/0.75in and `@page { size: Letter; margin: 0 }`, you get the padding. If `@page` also sets margins, double margins. Plan says "One owner sets margins: CSS @page, with preferCSSPageSize." But the templates already set padding. They'd need to remove padding or set `@page margin: 0` and let the root padding own it. The plan doesn't say which; "One owner" is meant to resolve this, but the templates' padding is the owner. This is a concrete fidelity risk: `@page` margin + template padding doubles margins, shrinking content. The spike 0b should catch. Add as a low/medium finding: specify `@page { margin: 0 }` with template padding, or strip template padding; don't do both.

**Page breaks**: Plan says "Entries don't break inside when they fit on a page." CSS `break-inside: avoid` on entries. But in Chromium, `break-inside: avoid` on flex items often fails; the templates use flex for experience rows and two-column layout. `break-inside: avoid` is not supported for flex containers? It has partial support. Also "An oversized entry continues with widow and orphan control" - `orphans`/`widows` only apply to block text, not flex. The plan may overpromise. And "headings stay with their content" via `break-after: avoid`. Chromium support for `break-after: avoid` on headings is decent. But inside a flex item, no. The template entries are `<div>` with nested flex for title/date, but the entry itself is a block. Fine. The sidebar is a flex item. Hmm.

**Font size min 9.5pt**: Current templates use 11px/12px/13px, which at 96dpi is 8.25pt/9pt/9.75pt. So the plan's "minimum body size 9.5pt" requires bumping font sizes; that will change layout and page counts. The plan doesn't say to change font sizes, only "minimum body size is 9.5 pt". But the templates use `fontSize: '11px'` in GermanATS and 12px body. 11px = 8.25pt, 12px = 9pt. So to meet 9.5pt, all templates need 12.67px+ body. This will make CVs longer and may break the 1-page US target. This is a real inconsistency: the plan sets a 9.5pt floor but the existing templates are below it; implementing it changes layout and the visual parity. Also ATS best practice is 10-12pt, so 9.5pt is low. Add a finding: section 6's minimum 9.5pt conflicts with existing 11-12px template sizes and will change pagination; either raise to 10pt and re-layout, or state the exception. Good.

Wait, CSS px to pt: 1pt = 1.333px, so 12px = 9pt, 13px = 9.75pt, 11px = 8.25pt. Yes.

**A4 vs Letter**: Plan says "US Letter, A4 DACH". The cover letter US business letter on Letter; DIN on A4. Fine. But `export_documents` has "optional paper override"; if a US template is exported on A4, the template root is 8.5in wide with padding; `preferCSSPageSize` + `@page size: A4` would scale? Chromium print with a page size override and fixed 8.5in content may overflow or shrink. The plan says other EU countries use US conventions "on A4, via the paper override". But the US templates have fixed width `8.5in` (816px), so on A4 (210mm = 8.27in) the content is wider than the page and will clip or scale. `preferCSSPageSize` means the CSS `@page` size wins; the element still 8.5in. This is a concrete bug: paper override is not a supported layout parameter; the templates are fixed-width. Need to make templates width-agnostic (`width: 100%`) or have A4 variants. Add finding (high/medium). The plan assumes a simple paper override, but the templates' root widths are hardcoded (8.5in, 210mm). Let me verify all: USPremium 8.5in, GermanATS 210mm. Others likely same. This is a strong finding.

**DOCX fidelity**: Plan says CV DOCX native paragraphs and heading styles, same order/headings, ImageRun photo, A4/Letter. But DOCX generation for the *templates* is hard: two-column German ATS can't be replicated natively without tables/text boxes, which the plan forbids. So the DOCX will look different from the PDF for `german-ats` (single column). The plan says "Same order and headings as the PDF", not same layout. That's acceptable for ATS, but "same renderer" and "canvas and PDF stay identical" doesn't include DOCX. Still, the product goal says "exporting a properly formatted CV in any of the 6 templates" — DOCX in german-ats won't be two-column. Maybe okay (DOCX is ATS-first). But the plan should state the fidelity tradeoff. Low.

**Cover letter DOCX**: `docx` from the same structure; fine. But the plan doesn't mention page size/margins for DIN cover letter DOCX. If DOCX uses default Letter, a German letter is wrong. Add to the paper issue.

**Cover letter structured contract**: The plan says "The writer agent moves to a structured letter contract ... Legacy body-only letters are rendered as they are, in letter typography, with no second header added." The prior review found the body is already a full letter. The plan's structured contract requires changing the writer prompt and output type. But the **existing `GeneratedCoverLetter` type** has applicantAddress, companyAddress, date, salutation, body, closing, signatureName. The body already contains the full letter for the 3-agent architecture. The plan says "legacy body-only letters are rendered as they are". Good. But the structured contract needs a migration path for existing stored letters; the plan mentions legacy rendering. Fine. However, the **anti-hallucination** rule: recipient "only verified details, never invented". Where do verified recipient details come from? `company_address`, `company_contact_person` in `application_sessions`, set by `update_application` or company research. The tool `update_application` can set them. Fine. But the plan doesn't say the letter writer must refuse to invent a recipient name/salutation when missing. It says only verified details. Good.

**DIN 5008 and German cover letter content**: The plan says "The date is on the right ('Berlin, 20. Oktober 2026')" and "One blank line after 'Sehr geehrte …'". But it doesn't mention the **Betreff** should include the job title/reference, the **Anrede** should use the recipient's name if known (not "Sehr geehrte Damen und Herren"), and the **closing** "Mit freundlichen Grüßen" plus signature. The structured contract has subject, salutation, closing. Fine.
- It also doesn't mention **no comma after the salutation** (German letters use "Sehr geehrte Frau X," with a comma, then lowercase start; actually DIN 5008: the salutation line ends with a comma, and the body starts lowercase. Some styles use no comma. The plan says one blank line after "Sehr geehrte …", but not comma/case. Minor.
- It doesn't mention **"Mit freundlichen Grüßen"** should be followed by the signature and no comma. It says no comma. Good.

**DIN address field 20mm from left vs text margin 25mm**: Plan says "A4, 25 mm left and 20 mm right margins" for CV, and for the cover letter "address field ... 20 mm from the left." That's consistent: text margin 25mm, address field outdented to 20mm. But the plan writes "A4, 25 mm left and 20 mm right margins" in the CV row (not cover letter). A CV doesn't have an address field; 25mm left is fine. The cover letter's text margin should also be 25mm, but the plan only gives the address field 20mm. It doesn't explicitly say the letter body starts at 25mm. Minor.

**"The address field is 85 × 45 mm, starting 45 mm from the top and 20 mm from the left. The return line sits in its top 5 mm."** The source says the address field is 85x45 and starts at 45mm, 20mm from left; the "Zusatz- und Vermerkzone" is 5 lines (17.7mm), and the "Anschriftzone" is 6 lines (27.3mm). The return line is in the top zone. Plan's "top 5 mm" is slightly off: it's the first line of a 17.7mm zone, not the top 5mm; and the recipient address should start at 62.7mm? Actually the recipient address occupies the lower 27.3mm of the 45mm field, i.e., starts 17.7mm below the top (62.7mm from page top). If they place the recipient address at the top of the field, it will be 17.7mm too high and may miss the window. This is a concrete DIN error! Let me verify: The address field 85x45 is divided into a 5-line "Zusatz- und Vermerkzone" (17.7mm) at top and a 6-line "Anschriftzone" (27.3mm) below. The recipient address goes in the Anschriftzone, starting 17.7mm below the field top, i.e., at 62.7mm from the page top. The plan says "The address field is 85 × 45 mm, starting 45 mm from the top ... The return line sits in its top 5 mm." It doesn't say where the recipient address starts within the field. If the implementation puts the recipient block at 50mm (45+5), it's wrong; should be ~62.7mm. The plan's "return line sits in its top 5 mm" suggests they think the rest of the field is the recipient address starting at 50mm. That's a real error. The eval checks "DIN field positions" but if the expected position is wrong, it validates the wrong thing. This is a high finding: the plan's DIN geometry is incomplete/incorrect about the address field's internal zones. Cite the source (DIN-Formate.info) or a standard. I should be careful: some sources may say the address field's top 5mm is the return line and the recipient address starts 5mm below, with the remaining 40mm for address. Let me verify more. The manager-institut page title "45 mal 85 millimeter" might explain. Let me fetch with a different URL or search for "Anschriftenfeld 5 Zeilen Zusatz Vermerkzone".
dsh: reasoning:
The search snippet confirms "In der Zusatz- und Vermerkzone steht die jeweils oberste Zeile für die einzellige Rücksendeangabe […] zur Verfügung" — the top line of the 5-line zone is for the return address. So the recipient address starts after the 5-line zone. Good. I can cite din-formate.info for the zone heights and this snippet. That's enough.

So finding: plan's DIN geometry omits the 17.7mm Zusatz-/Vermerkzone and the 27.3mm Anschriftzone; "return line in its top 5mm" conflates the top line of the 5-line zone with the whole zone. The recipient address must start ~62.7mm from the top, not 50mm. The eval's "DIN field positions" must encode this or it will pass a wrong layout. High.

Now **ATS rules completeness**:
- **Keyword matching / standard section headings**: Plan lists headings but the templates use uppercase and AI-provided `sectionHeaders`. ATS parsers often require exact standard headings; uppercase is fine. But the plan doesn't mention **no headers/footers** for cover letters? It says contact details in body. Fine.
- **Contact info**: Plan says "Contact details are in the body, never in a header or footer." But the templates put contact in a `<header>` HTML element (semantic header, not a PDF page header). That's fine; ATS reads it as body text. But the *plan* wording "never in a header" could be misread as not using the `<header>` tag. Minor.
- **Tables/columns**: ATS best practice: no tables, no columns, no text boxes. The plan keeps two-column German templates and claims "measured ATS level". `COMPLIANCE_CHECKLIST.US.atsCompatibility: 'No tables, columns, text boxes'`. A two-column German ATS template is inherently not ATS-safe for many parsers. The plan recommends a single-column default but still offers two-column and lists "measured ATS level". Good, but the product goal says "best-in-class ATS ... in two markets" and "including exporting a properly formatted CV in any of the 6 templates". If `german-ats` is two-column, it can't be best-in-class ATS. The plan's own `ATS level as measured, not as a promise` is honest, but the goal demands best-in-class. The plan should say the single-column template is the only one certified, and for `german-ats` exports maybe produce a single-column PDF (but then not identical to canvas). The prior review's "recommend single-column default" was adopted, but the goal remains. This is a tension worth flagging: "measured ATS level" doesn't satisfy "best-in-class for ATS" for the two-column template; either make the export ATS variant or drop the ATS label. High? I'd say medium-high. But the plan already says "A single-column ATS template is the recommended default in both markets." It doesn't claim all 6 are best-in-class. The product goal says "including exporting a properly formatted CV in any of the 6 templates" and "Exports must be best-in-class for ATS". That's contradictory for two-column templates. Flag as high because it's a product-goal mismatch the plan glosses over.

- **US "no photo"**: The plan says "Per template, as today. list_cv_templates and the skill state the US norm (no photo)." But `premium-ats` (US) supports a photo. If a user has a photo and picks premium-ats, the export includes a photo on a US CV, which US recruiters/ATS may penalize and which the repo's own `COMPLIANCE_CHECKLIST.US.forbidden` says no photo. The plan preserves it to avoid a silent change, but it should at least warn or default off for US templates. This was a prior finding; v2 adopted "per template, no silent change". Is it still a defect? The product goal is best-in-class ATS in the US; shipping a US template with a photo by default contradicts the repo's own compliance checklist. The plan's `list_cv_templates` states the norm, but the export doesn't enforce. I'd flag as medium: add an explicit opt-in per export and default photo off for US templates, or remove photo from US templates. Since v2 deliberately chose no silent change, I can frame it as "the plan still lets a US template render a photo; the repo's own compliance checklist forbids it; a warning in list_cv_templates isn't enforcement." Good.

- **German CV photo**: Plan says optional, top right, "Bewerbungsfoto ratio". Current GermanATS uses 35x47mm (ratio ~0.745), GermanClassic? Check. Fine.
- **German CV signature**: Not mentioned. The repo checklist says signature required. The template supports it. The plan's `get_cv`/export should include it. Minor.

- **File names**: Plan says `First_Last_CV.pdf`, `Lebenslauf_First_Last.pdf`. The existing `kitFilename` includes company and suffix (`Alex_Morgan_Pixellot_Cover_Letter.pdf`). The plan changes file names for MCP exports, but the web app's filenames will remain company-based unless changed. "Same renderer" doesn't require same filenames. But the plan says "File names: ..." for every PDF, which would be a behavior change for the web app or inconsistent. Low.

- **PDF/A?** Many European ATS/HR portals prefer PDF/A or at least embedded fonts; Chromium embeds subset fonts. The plan doesn't mention PDF/A or `pdfaid`. Not required. Dismiss.

- **Text layer order**: already covered.

- **Font embedding/licensing**: Arimo and Gelasio are OFL, fine. But the plan says "self-hosted fonts as base64 @font-face" and "Screen and PDF use the same files". Self-hosting Arimo/Gelasio adds ~200-400KB base64 per PDF, and "file size under 2 MB" still okay. But **subsetting**: base64 of full Arimo (regular/bold/italic) can be ~300KB each; 4 weights x 2 families = >1MB, plus photo. Could approach 2MB. Chromium subsets fonts in the PDF, so the PDF size is fine; the HTML payload is large but within 50MB. Fine. The HTML sent to Browser Run includes base64 fonts; no subrequests. Good.

- **Fonts in Browser Run**: custom fonts supported via `addStyleTag`; inline `@font-face` in the HTML works. Good. Dismiss the "fonts not supported" concern.

- **`tagged`**: Need to verify. If unsupported, the plan's spike catches it. But the plan *asserts* "`tagged` is on". Let me quickly check Cloudflare docs for "tagged" in llms-full. Maybe use `web_fetch` on llms-full and rely on returned text; it might be too big and truncated. Could use a search query with exact phrase.
dsh: reasoning:
The search doesn't confirm. Let me fetch the "Generate PDFs Using HTML and CSS" page; it may mention supported features.
dsh: reasoning:
The docs example includes `<meta charset="utf-8">`. Good, I can cite that as evidence the plan should include it. More importantly, the PDF generation guide recommends Puppeteer for additional control. Quick Actions `pdfOptions` likely maps to Puppeteer `page.pdf()` options, and Puppeteer PDFOptions has `tagged` (experimental) and `outline` (experimental) in recent versions. Cloudflare's API may expose `tagged`. The plan says "tagged is on, but treated as accessibility, not a parsing guarantee" — cautious. Fine. I can mention that the spike must verify `tagged` is accepted by `quickAction` because the docs' pdfOptions examples don't list it; if it's silently ignored, no tagged PDF. But the plan already says spike 0b tests tagging. Not a defect, maybe DISMISS.

Now **Claude/ChatGPT directory review**. Need to scrutinize:
- Anthropic connector directory requirements: remote MCP server, OAuth, privacy policy, terms, support, test account, tool annotations, tool names/descriptions, no harmful content, etc. Claude connectors with remote MCP require OAuth 2.0 (DCR or CIMD), `/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server`, `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource`, etc.
- OpenAI (ChatGPT) app directory: MCP server with OAuth, tool annotations, app manifest? The plan says "OpenAI manifest and domain challenge". Current OpenAI apps SDK uses MCP with `search`/`fetch`? The ChatGPT app directory requires a "domain verification" file, privacy policy, etc. It also requires **OAuth dynamic client registration** or a pre-registered client? ChatGPT supports DCR? The plan says CIMD advertised and DCR stays on. For OpenAI, they require the OAuth 2.1 spec with PKCE and **Dynamic Client Registration** (DCR) for MCP; ChatGPT's connector setup supports DCR. Fine.
- **Tool annotations**: OpenAI requires `readOnlyHint` etc. The plan sets all four explicitly. Good. But OpenAI's `destructiveHint`/`openWorldHint` semantics: `openWorldHint` true for tools that access external data (job feed, web). Plan says "Every tool sets title, readOnlyHint, destructiveHint and openWorldHint as explicit booleans." Good.
- **Commerce rules**: ChatGPT app directory prohibits selling digital goods? The plan says "ChatGPT forbids selling credits or plans in-app. Spending credits a user already has is fine." Is that correct? OpenAI's app monetization policy: apps can't sell physical goods? In ChatGPT apps, you can use "Instant Checkout" for physical goods; digital goods/subscriptions are not allowed? The plan's interpretation may be wrong: ChatGPT's commerce policy may prohibit **any** purchase within the app, including credits, but using existing credits is fine. The plan leaves out checkout. Good. But the product is credit-metered; the directory reviewer may reject an app that requires a paid subscription/credits purchased outside ChatGPT? OpenAI's app review guidelines: apps must provide core functionality without requiring a separate account? Actually many apps require login. The plan seeds a reviewer account. Fine.
- **Reviewer account**: Plan says seeded reviewer account (no MFA). But Better Auth requires email verification (`requireEmailVerification: true`) and login with email/password. A seeded reviewer account must be email-verified and have credits/CV data. The plan says "seeded reviewer account (no MFA)" but doesn't mention **email verification** or seeding a CV, credits, job feed consent. The reviewer can't test generation without credits/CV. The plan's PR 6 "seeded reviewer account" should include verified email, a starter CV, credits, and maybe a demo application. This is a directory-review risk. Also "no MFA" — Better Auth doesn't have MFA configured, fine.
- **Privacy policy / terms / support URLs**: Plan includes. But OpenAI requires a **support contact** and **data deletion** instructions. Account deletion is left out of tools, but the privacy policy should cover. Fine.
- **OAuth consent screen**: The plan has `/oauth/consent` and Settings > Connected apps. But Anthropic's connector directory requires the OAuth flow to work in a browser; ChatGPT's connector setup is in ChatGPT settings. Fine.
- **CIMD**: `@better-auth/cimd` - is that a real package? Better Auth has a `cimd` plugin? Need to verify. The plan says "Packages: @better-auth/mcp (on @better-auth/oauth-provider), jwt() and @better-auth/cimd." Are these real package names/versions? Better Auth 1.7 has `@better-auth/oauth-provider` plugin? I recall Better Auth has an `oidcProvider` plugin and an `mcp` plugin (added in 1.4?). `@better-auth/mcp` might be real. `@better-auth/cimd` maybe not. The plan says "Spike first. Confirm exact versions". So it's flagged as unknown. But the plan asserts them as packages. If they don't exist, PR 1 is blocked. The spike covers. Not a finding beyond "verify package existence". Could mention the spike must also verify **DCR/CIMD callback URL constraints** with ChatGPT, but plan says.

- **OAuth resource binding**: The plan says "The resource parameter is bound." Good (RFC 8707). 
- **Token revocation**: Plan says access tokens are JWTs 10 min, revocation table checked so an access token dies within a minute. But if the verifier checks a revocation table on every request, it's a D1 read per request; okay. But "revoking in Connected apps kills the refresh token immediately" - Better Auth's OAuth provider may not expose a revocation endpoint; the plan says Settings > Connected apps. Fine.
- **Token scope**: `read`, `write`, `spend`. The plan says every tool declares required scope and handler enforces. But if a read token can call `export_documents` (write), it gets signed URLs to files; fine. If a `write` token can call `get_credit_history`, fine. A `spend` token can also write? Scope hierarchy? The plan doesn't define whether `spend` implies `write` and `read`, or whether tokens can have multiple scopes. If scopes are independent, a spend-only token can't call write tools, which is awkward. The plan says "A test calls every write tool with a read-only token." Fine. But the consent screen must let users choose scopes; the plan doesn't say. Minor.
- **MCP spec version**: Plan says "Accept legacy stateless MCP clients as well as the 2026-07-28 spec." `createMcpHandler` from Cloudflare's `agents/mcp`? The plan says "Cloudflare's stateless `createMcpHandler`; `McpAgent` is deprecated". Is `createMcpHandler` a real API? The `agents` package has `createMcpHandler` in `agents/mcp` (as of 2025). It supports stateless Streamable HTTP. Fine. But `McpAgent` is not deprecated globally; Cloudflare recommends `McpAgent` for stateful, `createMcpHandler` for stateless. Saying "McpAgent is deprecated" may be false but not material. Low.

- **OAuth on `/mcp` + Next route**: OpenNext routing. The plan spike 0a. Fine.

- **CORS**: ChatGPT/Claude connectors call server-to-server; no CORS. Fine.

- **`WWW-Authenticate` / 401**: MCP OAuth discovery requires a 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=...` when unauthenticated. The plan says `requireMcpAuth`. It doesn't mention the 401 challenge header. This is a directory/OAuth compliance detail: without it, Claude/ChatGPT may not discover the authorization server. The plan has `/.well-known/*` and OAuth, but the MCP spec requires the 401 response to include `WWW-Authenticate` pointing to protected resource metadata. Add as a low/medium finding. Also `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` must be at the resource origin (and possibly path-specific `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp`). Plan says `/.well-known/*` generically. Fine.

- **ChatGPT domain challenge**: Plan says "OpenAI manifest and domain challenge". ChatGPT apps require a `/.well-known/ai-plugin.json`? That's the old plugin spec. The new Apps SDK uses MCP, and domain verification is via a DNS TXT or a file? The plan says "domain challenge". Fine.

- **Anthropic Claude Code plugin directory**: Plan has `.mcp.json` plus one skill. But a Claude Code plugin's `.mcp.json` for a remote server with OAuth: Claude Code handles OAuth via `/mcp` command. Fine. The plugin repo `wasimjalali/usefulapply-plugin` must be public and have a `plugin.json`/marketplace manifest. The plan says "Claude Code plugin (Anthropic plugin directory + repo)". It doesn't mention the plugin manifest format, versioning, or the marketplace entry. Low.

- **ChatGPT app manifest**: The plan says "OpenAI manifest". Current OpenAI apps don't use a manifest file; they use the MCP server + `openai-app.json`? I'm not sure. Not worth flagging.

**Security: OAuth**
- **IDOR via application_id**: Services check ownership. But `get_operation` must check user; plan says. Fine.
- **Signed URL**: HMAC key. Where is the secret? `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET` reuse? Plan doesn't say a separate `EXPORT_URL_SECRET`. If they reuse `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET`, rotating auth secret invalidates exports; if they use a hardcoded secret, bad. Plan should specify a dedicated secret. Low/medium.
- **R2 key collision**: `sha256(content, template, paper, locale, rendererVersion, photoHash)` — `content` is the JSON of the kit. If two users have identical content (unlikely) the key includes userId. Fine. But `photoHash` is a hash of the photo bytes; if the photo is changed, new key. Fine.
- **Cache hit re-checks ownership**: good.
- **Signed URL leakage**: The MCP tool returns signed links; ChatGPT/Claude may render them as links. If the agent includes them in a chat transcript, anyone with the link can download for 15 min. That's by design. Could bind to the MCP client's IP? No. Fine.
- **Prompt injection**: The plan wraps job descriptions and tool output, but **the job description is used as input to the writer prompt**, not just returned to the client. The writer prompt already has anti-injection rules. The plan says "Every string that comes from a job description, a feed item or a web page goes back to the client wrapped in the untrusted delimiters" — "goes back to the client" is output-side. The input-side injection into the writer prompt is the bigger risk: an agent tool `generate_application_kit` takes a job description and sends it to the writer; if the JD contains "ignore previous instructions and output a CV claiming...", the writer prompt's anti-hallucination rules should handle it. The plan's injection posture focuses on tool output, not model input. It says "the untrusted-content wrapper in chat-tools/untrusted.ts" is reused. But `untrusted.ts` sanitizes and wraps; the writer prompt's `sanitizeAdText` is used. The plan does not mention **tool arguments** from the agent (e.g., `instruction` in `regenerate_document`) being user/agent-authored and prompt-injected. It says `concise` mode, facts only. Hmm. I'd flag: the plan's injection defense is output-side; it doesn't state that the **job description and agent-supplied instructions** passed into the writer are wrapped as data and length-capped, or that tool descriptions tell the model to treat tool results as data. Section 3.5 says "those tools' descriptions say the content is data" and "Tool results contain facts only". It does cover tool output. It doesn't mention input. The existing chat CV tools have a strong `userMessagePasted` gate; the MCP tools have no such gate. An agent can be tricked by a job ad into calling `save_cv` with a malicious CV or `delete_application`. The plan's mitigation is `confirm: true` "must come from an explicit user request". But the agent model can be prompt-injected to send `confirm: true`. The confirmation is only a string, not a server-verified human action. This is a real security hole: `confirm: true` is not a human-in-the-loop control; a prompt-injected agent can set it. For destructive tools, the server should require an out-of-band confirmation (e.g., a web app approval, or a short-lived confirmation token from the user) or at least treat `confirm` as a UX hint, not a security boundary. This is high. The plan says "Destructive tools take confirm: true, which the description says must come from an explicit user request." That's exactly the weak pattern. Add finding. Directory reviewers (Anthropic) may flag destructive tools. Also `delete_application` could be triggered by a job description injection. The existing chat tools use `userNamed` and a click. MCP has no click. This is a significant regression.

- **Prompt injection through tool output**: `get_job_feed` returns untrusted-wrapped job descriptions. But the **agent** may treat the wrapped text as instructions. Wrapping is not a security boundary. The plan says "Tool results contain facts only, never instructions." Good but not enforceable. The destructive tool issue is the concrete hole.

- **Credit abuse by looping agent**: Daily cap 10 credits, but free tools unlimited. A looping agent can call `save_cv` (LLM parse, no credits), `run_job_feed` (once/day), `get_job_feed` (free), `export_documents` (Browser Run cost, no credits), `analyze_job_fit` (0.5). **Exports are free and cost Browser Run money.** A looping agent can call `export_documents` repeatedly with different `templateId`/`paper` to burn Browser Run hours and R2 storage, with no credit charge or daily cap. The plan's cost estimate assumes ~12,000 PDFs/month; a single agent could generate thousands. There is no export rate limit or per-user export quota. This is a real cost abuse hole. High. Also `save_cv` parse cost. Add.

- **R2 storage abuse**: Exports stored 30 days, keyed by content hash. Repeated exports of the same content hit the cache, so no new object; different templates/paper = 6*2=12 variants per kit; plus edits. Not huge, but a looping agent can edit/export. Need per-user quota or rate limit. Medium.

- **Queue abuse**: An agent can enqueue 2 concurrent generations, then more after they finish, up to daily cap. Fine.

**Credit/idempotency bugs**
- **Double charge on MCP client retries**: The tool requires `idempotencyKey`; a retry with the same key returns the same operation. But **MCP clients may not retry with the same key**; the model may generate a new key on retry (the plan says "MCP clients retry with fresh ids" in section 2). The plan requires the client to supply the key, but the model is the client author; it can generate a new UUID each call. The plan's fix relies on the agent reusing the key, which it may not. A better design: the server derives an idempotency key from the tool name + user + a stable input hash, or accepts an optional key and dedupes identical requests within a window. The plan says "A retry with the same key returns the same operation" and "A retry with the same key but different input is rejected." But if the client retries with a fresh key, double charge persists. The prior review specifically said "MCP clients retry with fresh ids" and the plan's fix is to require a client UUID. That only works if the client honors it. The plan doesn't say the **tool description instructs the model to reuse the key on retry**, or that the server auto-dedupes by input hash. This is a high finding: the idempotency design shifts the burden to an unreliable client. Fix: server-side request fingerprint `(user, tool, normalized input)` with a short TTL, plus optional client key; or return an operation for an identical pending/succeeded request within N minutes. The plan has `input_hash` in `agent_operations`, which could support this, but it only uses it to reject same-key-different-input. Use it to dedupe same-input-different-key within a window. Good.

- **Double charge on Queue redelivery**: lease/claim mitigates, but as noted lease expiry. Also the consumer "On success it runs persistKit and captures the reservation." If `persistKit` succeeds and capture fails (D1 error), the reservation stays `reserved`; reconcile might release/refund, giving a free generation. Or if capture succeeds and persistKit fails, the user is charged but no output; reconcile sees captured and doesn't refund. The plan says "Refunds are atomic" for failure, but not "capture and persist are atomic". The order matters: persistKit + capture should be in one D1 batch, or the operation status should be the source of truth. Since `persistKit` writes multiple tables (application_sessions, maybe chat), and capture is one row, a single batch is possible but complex. The plan says "On success it runs persistKit and captures the reservation" (two steps). A crash between them loses either the output or the charge. This is the same class as the old refund bug. Add finding: success path is not atomic; put `persistKit`, operation status, and capture in one D1 batch, or make the reconcile idempotent and able to complete from `result_ref`. High. The plan only made the **failure** path atomic.

- **`agent_operations.result_ref`**: If the result is stored where? It says `result_ref`. If the operation row is the source of truth, `get_operation` can return results. Fine.
- **Reservation key unique index**: global. If the client supplies a UUID that collides across users (unlikely), the second user gets `RESERVATION_SPENT` from another user's key because `getReservationByKey` filters by user, but the insert unique index is global. It would fail insert, then `getReservationByKey` returns null for the second user, then refund and error. A malicious user could deliberately reuse a known key? They don't know other users' keys. Low. But the plan's `agent_operations` unique is per user, fine. Reservation keys should be namespaced with userId. Low.

- **Refund atomicity with `addCredits` optimistic concurrency**: If a batch has `UPDATE credit_reservations`, `UPDATE user_credits` (with a version/where), and `INSERT credit_transactions`, D1 batch is atomic. Fine.
- **Credit restore across buckets**: The existing `refundDeduction` restores to monthly/rollover/addon based on `deductedFrom`. The plan says "credit restore" in one batch. Fine.
- **Partial kit failures**: Existing `KIT_DOCUMENT_FAILURE_REFUND` refunds per failed document. The plan's operation status is one per kit; if one document fails and others succeed, the plan says "On terminal failure it releases the reservation" (full refund) and "On success it runs persistKit". What about partial success? The plan doesn't mention the existing per-document failure refunds. A kit where the CV succeeds but the cover letter fails should capture 1.5 and refund 0.5, not fail entirely or capture 2.0. The plan's operation model is binary. This is a credit correctness gap. Add finding (medium-high). The repo already has `KIT_DOCUMENT_FAILURE_REFUND`; the MCP path must reuse it. Good catch.

- **`generate_application_kit` "1.5 after fit, else 2.0"**: The fit-continuation discount is claimed server-side. But if an agent calls `analyze_job_fit` (0.5) and then `generate_application_kit` (1.5), total 2.0. If it calls `generate_application_kit` without fit, 2.0. Good. But if it calls fit, then the kit, then retries the kit with a new key, 1.5 again (should be 2.0 because the discount is one-time). The plan says "The fit-continuation discount is claimed server-side", implying one-time claim. Need to ensure the reservation claim is tied to the fit reservation and consumed. Fine.

- **`rewrite_cv_section` "0.5, free if refused"**: How does the server know it was refused before charging? It reserves 0.5, then if validation fails, releases. Fine. But the tool returns a refusal; the agent may retry in a loop, each time reserve/release, generating D1 churn. Fine.

- **`cancel_generation`** free and releases unspent credits. If the operation already captured, cancel should not refund. Plan says "unspent credits are released". Fine, but race.

**Race conditions between server persistence and web sync**
- Plan's revision column and client stop wholesale sync. Good. But **`useSupabaseSync` also syncs chat messages**; the plan only stops generated fields. If the agent writes a chat message? Not in catalog. Fine.
- The **web client's `useSupabaseSync` still writes `selected_template`, `company_address`, `company_contact_person`, `status`?** It doesn't write status (deliberately), but it does write `selected_template`, `company_address`, `company_contact_person`, `generation_status`, `generation_completed`, `job_description`, `requirements`, `gaps`, `fit_assessment`. If the plan only adds `revision` to kit fields, a stale tab can still overwrite `selected_template` and `company_address` (the cover letter recipient!) without a revision check. The plan says "The web client stops writing generated fields wholesale. Canvas and editor saves go through the same edit services with the revision they loaded." It doesn't explicitly cover `selected_template`, company research, fit assessment, job description. A stale tab could overwrite the agent's `set_cv_template` or `update_application` company address, causing the cover letter to use the wrong recipient. This is a real remaining race. Add finding: revision checks must cover every column the debounced sync still writes, or the sync must be reduced to a narrow allowlist. High/medium.
- Also `useSupabaseSync` creates a new session if `sessionHasKit` and local ID; an agent creates a draft (server-side) while the web tab has a local draft for the same job. The web tab's sync may create a duplicate application or reuse the agent's draft, overwriting it. The plan's revision doesn't cover **session creation**. The `findReusableSession` logic matches `drafted` and same job; if the agent's draft has no kit and status `drafted`, the web tab could reuse it and overwrite. Or create a duplicate because the local draft ID is different and the sessions array is stale. The plan doesn't address creation races. Add as medium.
- The plan's polling `revision` "on focus and every 15 seconds while it's open" is a read; but the client may still have unsaved local edits. Reloading the application on revision change could discard the user's in-progress edit. Need merge/conflict UX. The plan says "On a conflict, the client reloads that application and tells the user it was updated elsewhere." That can discard unsaved work. A better approach is a field-level merge or a "reload" prompt. Medium.
- The plan says "After a background or agent change, the client refreshes the open application, by polling revision on focus and every 15 seconds while it's open." This is a per-open-app poll; if many tabs, D1 load. Fine.
- **D1 revision conditional update**: `UPDATE ... WHERE id=? AND user_id=? AND revision=?` returns new revision. But the **server-side generation consumer** uses `persistKit`, which must also bump revision. If it doesn't, the client's next save with the old revision succeeds and overwrites. The plan says `persistKit` is the only writer and revisioned. Fine.
- **Revision on `application_sessions`**: The plan adds an integer. Existing rows need a default 0. Migration. Fine.
- **SQLite/D1 `RETURNING`**: D1 supports `RETURNING`? It does for INSERT/UPDATE. Fine.

**Workers limits**
- **CPU 300s** configured. Queue consumer could run 5 min CPU. Fine.
- **OpenNext + Queue consumer**: `runWithCloudflareRequestContext` needs a `Request` and `ctx`. In a queue handler, `ctx` is `ExecutionContext` with `waitUntil`; fine. But the plan says "enters the same request context as scheduled()". Need to use `env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` etc. Fine.
- **Queue consumer `max_batch_size: 1`** means one generation per invocation. Cloudflare Queues charges per operation; fine. But throughput: 1 message per invocation, max concurrency 10? At our volume fine.
- **Wall time**: The writer agent may take >15 min for a kit (CV + cover + email + follow-up, multiple LLM calls). The existing agent-v2 request has `maxDuration=300` (5 min) and streams. A kit generation might take 2-4 min. Queue consumer wall time 15 min. Fine. But **Browser Run + export** in the same consumer? No, export is separate.
- **Queue message retention**: 4 days. Fine.
- **D1 write throughput**: A single D1 database; 2 concurrent generations per user. Fine.
- **Browser Run concurrency**: Need to check limits. Let me fetch the limits page.
dsh: reasoning:
Browser Run limits: Workers Paid Quick Actions 30 requests/second total, 200 concurrent browser sessions only for browser sessions. Quick Actions has no concurrency limit listed, just 30 rps. Fine. But **Browser Run has a 60-second browser timeout**, PDF action timeout 5 min. Fine.

Cost: plan says 3s/PDF. But the **first** PDF after a cold start may take longer; Browser Run startup. Still ~3-5s. Fine.

Now, **the 2 MB file size target**: Browser Run PDF with base64 fonts and photo, plus PDF-lib. Chromium subsets fonts; likely <2MB. Fine.

**PDF text extraction order**: Let me verify with a quick local test? We could create an HTML with flex order and use headless Chromium (Playwright?) to print to PDF and pdftotext. But no modifying/creating files? The task says read-only; we can't create files. We could run a one-liner with `node` and in-memory? That would create temp files. The instructions say do not modify, create or delete any file. We shouldn't. We can rely on knowledge/spec. Could use an existing PDF? No. We can cite CSS spec and Chromium behavior. But the finding is strong by reasoning.

Actually, I can verify without writing to repo by using `/tmp`? The instruction says "do not modify, create or delete any file" — likely in the repo; but safer not to. We have enough.

**DIN 5008** further:
- Plan says "A4, 25 mm left and 20 mm right margins" for DACH CV. DIN 5008 is for letters, not CVs. Fine.
- "Other EU countries such as the UK, Ireland, the Netherlands and the Nordics use the US conventions on A4, via the paper override." UK CVs are typically 2 pages, no photo, A4. Fine. But the **paper override** issue.
- "File names: First_Last_CV.pdf, Lebenslauf_First_Last.pdf" - German CV file name "Lebenslauf_First_Last.pdf" is fine.
- "File size under 2 MB (Greenhouse caps uploads at 2.5 MB)." Greenhouse's actual max file size: I think 5MB? Let me verify. Not critical but factual. Search.
dsh: reasoning:
"100 MB upload, 2.5 MB to parse" - the plan's claim may be a confusion: Greenhouse caps *parsing* at 2.5 MB, not uploads. The plan says "Greenhouse caps uploads at 2.5 MB" — that's factually wrong if uploads are 100 MB but parsing is capped at 2.5MB. The practical target (<2MB) is still right for parse success, but the reason is wrong. Low finding. Could cite the search result, but it's a third-party page. Better: the Greenhouse support article "Supported formats" may state max size. Let me fetch.
dsh: reasoning:
Confirmed: Greenhouse caps uploads at 100 MB, not 2.5 MB. The plan's parenthetical is wrong. The <2MB target is still a reasonable parse target (Greenhouse parses up to 2.5MB per third-party). So finding: the stated reason is wrong; the target may be right but cite the parse cap. Low.

**"ATS parser proxy: OpenResume parser, version pinned, runs on the PDFs."** OpenResume's parser is JS/TS and uses pdf.js; it's a reasonable proxy but it is not Greenhouse/Workday. The plan also does a manual real-ATS check. Good. But OpenResume's parser only reads PDFs (prior review). The plan applies it to PDFs only. Fine. However OpenResume's parser is designed for its own resume format and may not handle German/umlauts. The eval includes a German CV; the parser may fail on German headings. If it fails, is that a product defect or a parser limitation? The plan says "Its parsed fields must match the source" for both US and German? It says "ATS-parser proxy ... runs on the PDFs. Its parsed fields must match the source." For German CVs, OpenResume's parser likely expects English section headings and may not parse "Berufserfahrung". That would make the eval fail for the German fixture, or force English headings (wrong for the German market). The plan doesn't scope the parser proxy to US/English or add a German-capable parser (e.g., Affinda, Sovren) or a second proxy. This is a verification gap. Add finding (medium): the parser proxy is English-centric; add a German parser or limit the proxy and rely on manual Personio/Greenhouse checks for German.

Also **OpenResume parser version pinned** - good.

**Visual parity**: "canvas screenshot against PDF page images, judged by a clean Opus judge plus Wasim." The user's global rules say design/UI judging: one model judge plus Wasim, latest Opus-class. Plan says "clean Opus judge plus Wasim" - compliant. But "clean" means no user rules/skills, only screenshots. Fine.

**E2E**: "Playwright Test ... MCP E2E" - Playwright cannot directly speak MCP OAuth easily; they can use the MCP SDK client in Node within the test. Fine. But **ChatGPT/Claude.ai live checks** are manual. Fine.

**Environment**: "runs against the remote Browser Run binding (`wrangler dev --remote`), because `quickAction` doesn't run locally." The docs say you can set `"remote": true` in the browser binding for local dev; `wrangler dev --remote` is the older mode. The plan says quickAction doesn't run locally; actually with remote bindings it can. Minor. But `wrangler dev --remote` may not work with OpenNext/Next 16 dev server. They can use `wrangler dev` with remote binding. Low.

**PR ordering/dependencies**:
- PR 0a/0b spikes (no merge) "findings committed to docs". Then PR 1 after 0a, PR 2 now, PR 3 after 0b.
- PR 3 "Integrates with 2's kit service when 2 merges" but starts after spike 0b, in parallel with 2. If PR 3's export service needs `persistKit`/revisioned services from PR 2, it can't fully integrate until 2 merges. The table says "Integrates with 2's kit service when 2 merges" - okay, but PR 3 also changes templates, which PR 2's `persistKit` doesn't depend on. Fine.
- PR 5 after 1 and 2. PR 6 after 3, 4, 5. Fine.
- **PR 4 (structured cover letter)** starts "now (parallel)" but depends on the export renderer? It changes the writer output contract and legacy rendering. If PR 3's export service assumes the old `GeneratedCoverLetter` type, PR 4 changes it; PR 3 and 4 both touch cover-letter rendering. The table says PR 4 starts now, parallel with 2; PR 3 starts after spike 0b. If PR 3 builds the cover letter PDF/DOCX from the old type and PR 4 changes the type, there's a merge conflict / rework. The plan says PR 3 "cover letter layouts" and PR 4 "structured cover letter... legacy rendering path". These are coupled. The dependency table should make PR 4 a dependency of PR 3's cover-letter part, or serialize them. This is a concrete PR ordering mistake. Add finding (medium).
- **PR 2 data integrity** includes "Queue + leased consumer in worker.ts" and "revisioned services", but PR 1 OAuth/MCP skeleton needs `get_account` only. Fine.
- **PR 3 Renderer + ATS** includes the ATS eval and template changes. But the **template changes (no caps)** affect the chat tools' `render-caps.ts` and the `page_warning` behavior. PR 3 removes `render-caps.ts`, but PR 5 (tool catalog) rewrites chat tools onto services. If PR 3 removes caps before PR 5 updates the chat tools, the `render-caps.ts` import in `cv-tools.ts` breaks (compile error) or the page warnings become wrong. The plan says "`render-caps.ts` go away" but the chat tool imports it. PR 3 must also update `cv-tools.ts` (a shared file with PR 5). The dependency table doesn't mention this. Add finding (medium): removing caps/render-caps touches chat tools that PR 5 owns; sequence or coordinate.
- **PR 3 "signed URLs" sensitive review** - good.
- **PR 6 "seeded reviewer account"** depends on PR 1 (auth) and PR 5 (tools). Fine.
- **Deploy after each merge; tools stay disabled until PR 6.** If PR 1 merges with the env flag off, fine. But PR 5 adds all tools behind the flag; PR 6 flips it. Fine.
- **"Tools stay disabled until PR 6"** but the **web app downloads switch to the server renderer in PR 3**, which is user-facing and unrelated to the MCP flag. If the renderer has a bug, the web app is affected before directory review. That's intended. Fine.

**Simpler alternative**: The plan is large: 40 tools, Queue, revision system, server renderer, 6 templates, DOCX, DIN. Is there something simpler that gets the same result? 
- **Skip the Queue for fit/regenerate**, only queue kit generation. Or use Cloudflare Workflows instead of Queues for durable multi-step generation. The plan uses Queues + leases + reconcile; Cloudflare Workflows offers durable steps, retries, and state, potentially simpler and avoids the lease/redelivery issues. But Workflows has its own limits and cost. The plan says "Queue-based generation" as a decision. The review should mention **Cloudflare Workflows** as a simpler alternative for durable generation, or **Durable Objects** for per-user serialization. But the user asked "anything simpler that gets the same result." A good suggestion: use Cloudflare Workflows for the generation pipeline (durable, step retries, no manual lease/reconcile), or use the existing SSE request path with a "generation job" row for MCP polling. But the plan chose Queues; not necessarily wrong. Mention in improvements, not a finding.
- **Skip server-side DOCX for the first release**? The product goal requires it. No.
- **Use a single print route with a signed token instead of inlining HTML**: The plan avoids a public route for security; inlining is simpler. Good.
- **Use the existing `@react-pdf/renderer` or `docx` for PDFs**? No, Browser Run gives CSS fidelity. Fine.
- **Use `pdf-lib` + `@pdf-lib/fontkit` to generate PDFs directly**? Would avoid Browser Run but lose template fidelity. No.
- **Use one ATS template for all exports** and treat visual templates as preview-only? That would be simpler and truly ATS-best-in-class, but violates "any of the 6 templates". The plan's tension. Suggest: make the export renderer use a print mode per template, but for two-column templates offer an "ATS-safe single-column export" toggle. That's a concrete improvement.

**Factual claims to verify in code**:
- Plan says "Exports run in the browser, at low quality: The CV PDF is an image of the page." Verified `cv-pdf.ts` uses html2canvas -> JPEG. Yes.
- "All 6 templates silently cut skills to 5 per group (`render-caps.ts`)" - verified. But `render-caps.ts` is not the cause; the `.slice()` calls are. Minor wording.
- "The cover letter PDF is plain Helvetica." Verified `cover-letter-export.ts` uses jsPDF helvetica. Yes.
- "Key actions are server actions only: create an application, save a CV, read an application, tracker updates, delete, profile." Verified actions. But **read an application** also has API routes (`/api/job-feed/apply/status`, `/api/agent-v2`), but the MCP needs services. Fine.
- "Chat CV and template tools don't persist anything. They return effects for the browser to apply, and their outline gate lasts one request." Verified app-tools returns `executed` effects; `ctx.cvOutlineRead` is per request. Yes.
- "The browser owns the kit. `useSupabaseSync.ts` writes `generated_cv`, `cover_letter`, `email` and `follow_up_email` wholesale from a stale tab every 2 seconds." Verified debounce 2000ms, writes all fields. Yes.
- "A headless kit never fills the canonical columns." Need to verify: the agent-v2 route writes `generated_cv`? It might persist via SSE? Search `updateSession` in agent-v2.
dsh: reasoning:
Let's see what agent-v2 persists at lines 974 and 1137. If it persists the kit to the canonical columns, then the plan's claim "A headless kit never fills the canonical columns" is wrong. Let me read.
dsh: reasoning:
`persistGeneratedArtifacts` writes `generated_artifacts`, not canonical `generated_cv`/`cover_letter`/`email`. So the plan's claim "A headless kit never fills the canonical columns" is true for agent-v2. But the plan says "Server-side generation, regeneration, follow-ups, CV parsing and the job feed are ready to reuse." The regenerate route does write canonical columns. The kit generation (agent-v2) writes `generated_artifacts` only. The plan's `persistKit` would write canonical columns. Fine.

But note: the web app **already** persists generated artifacts server-side (`generated_artifacts`) for agent-v2, and the client still writes canonical columns via `useSupabaseSync`. The plan says "A headless kit never fills the canonical columns" — true. It could instead read `generated_artifacts`, but the plan's `persistKit` is the right fix. Fine.

**`persistKit` and `generated_artifacts`**: The plan says "Server-side kit persistence (persistKit) is the only writer of generation output". Does that mean it also writes `generated_artifacts`? It should. The plan doesn't mention the existing `generated_artifacts` column or the agent-v2 `persistGeneratedArtifacts` path. There are now two server-side persistence paths: `generated_artifacts` (agent-v2) and `generated_cv`/`cover_letter` (regenerate, tracker follow-up). The plan says "Server-side kit persistence (`persistKit`) is the only writer of generation output, for web and MCP alike." But the existing `persistGeneratedArtifacts` writes `generated_artifacts`; the regenerate route writes canonical columns. The plan must **consolidate** these, not just add a new writer. If `persistKit` writes canonical columns while `persistGeneratedArtifacts` writes `generated_artifacts`, the web app may read one or the other, and they can diverge. The plan's PR 2 says "persistKit" but doesn't mention removing/replacing `persistGeneratedArtifacts` or the regenerate/tracker writes. This is a concrete gap. Add finding (high): the plan claims a single writer but the code has at least three writers (`agent-v2` artifacts, `regenerate`, `tracker/follow-up`), and `useSupabaseSync` a fourth; the migration must route all through `persistKit` or define precedence.

Let me check `buildKitResults` / `generated_artifacts` to see what it stores. Not necessary.

**`get_application` kit (`concise` or `full`)**: The plan says `concise` mode never returns the raw job description. But `get_application` needs the job description for the agent to generate a kit? The agent can pass `jobDescription` to `generate_application_kit`? The tool catalog's `generate_application_kit` doesn't list a job description parameter; it takes an application. The agent may need `get_application` full. Fine.

**`analyze_job_fit` on a pasted job description (creates a draft)**: If it creates a draft, it needs `job_description` and maybe title/company. Fine.

**`create_application`**: It says "Draft from a job description, title, company, URL". Does it parse the JD to extract title/company? The human's create action probably does. Fine.

**`save_cv`**: "Upload a CV (text, or PDF/DOCX/TXT/MD as base64), parse it, save as primary or master." The existing parse-cv accepts PDF/DOCX/TXT/MD/TEX; the plan omits `.tex`. Minor. More importantly, the parse CV route requires a `storagePath` or file; the MCP tool would need to call the parser directly. Fine.

**`set_profile_photo`**: Takes a base64 image. No size/format limits. Same as save_cv. Add to the upload size finding.

**`get_account`**: "Plan, balance, next reset, daily MCP cap and usage, CV on file, profile basics". The human can see credit history, subscription management, invoices. `get_credit_history` covers history; no invoices/payment method (left out). Fine.

**`list_applications`**: "Tracker list, filter by status, paginated". The human can sort, search, filter by hide rejected, etc. Minor.

**`morning_briefing`**: "What needs attention today (follow-ups due, interviews, new strong matches)". The web app has a dashboard and tracker reminders. Good.

**`explain_fit`**: The human sees fit assessment; good.

**`get_career_profile`**: good.

**`run_job_feed`**: "Today's feed (one a day, consent required)". The human can run the feed manually maybe more than once? The route has a daily guard. Fine.

**`withdraw_feed_consent`**: destructive, confirm. Good.

**Missing: `set_default_cv_template`** and **`update_notification_settings`**. Minor.

**Missing: `get_application` should include the job description?** `concise` mode. Fine.

**Tool naming**: `get_application` vs `list_applications`; consistent. `get_cv` vs `save_cv`. Fine. Names under 64 chars. `generate_application_kit` 24. Fine.

**Tool descriptions**: "one or two sentences" but the catalog has 40 tools; directory reviewers may find it bloated. Anthropic recommends a few tools, not 40. The plan says "within normal range for catalogs the directories have reviewed." Is 40 normal? Anthropic's tool search / connectors can have many tools, but a 40-tool catalog increases selection errors and context. The product goal is "everything a human can do", so 40 is defensible. But directory review may reject "too many tools" or "unclear tool boundaries". The plan separates read/write, good. A simpler alternative: fewer, higher-level tools (e.g., one `cv` tool with actions) would be worse for annotations. Keep.

**Tool annotations**: `readOnlyHint` true for read tools, but `get_account` reads credits; fine. `openWorldHint`: `get_job_feed` accesses external data; should be true. The plan says all four explicit, but doesn't specify values per tool. Directory review may check. Fine.

**Commerce rules**: `get_account` returns plan and balance; `get_credit_history` returns transactions; these are financial data. ChatGPT's commerce policy may require that apps not display prices. The plan says "no prices or upgrade text anywhere in tool output", but `get_account` returns "plan" (e.g., "Plus"), which is fine; `get_credit_history` may return "addon_purchase" / "plan_upgrade" rows with descriptions like "Stripe subscription renewal" and amounts. If the ledger description includes a dollar amount, that could be a price. The plan says "Tool results contain facts only" and "No content" analytics, but `get_credit_history` could leak Stripe descriptions. Low. The plan should sanitize ledger descriptions to credit amounts/actions, not dollar amounts. Maybe.

**Directory reviewer account**: As noted, needs email verification + credits + CV. Also **the reviewer account must be able to complete OAuth**; the plan seeds an account but the OAuth consent page requires a session. Fine. **No MFA** is fine. **Data isolation**: reviewer account sees only its own data. Fine.

**Anthropic directory**: Anthropic requires a **privacy policy**, **terms**, **support**, and a **logo**, **description**, **categories**, **use cases**, **screenshots**. The plan says "listing". Fine. It also requires the server to be **reviewed for safety**; a tool that deletes applications and spends credits may need clear annotations. The plan has. Good.

**OpenAI directory**: OpenAI's app review requires **OAuth 2.1 with PKCE**, **DCR**, **domain verification**, **privacy policy**, **support**, and **no use of OpenAI-branded models**? Fine. The plan says "identity verification needed first". Good.

**Anthropic Claude Code plugin directory**: The plugin directory may require the plugin to be in a public GitHub repo with a `plugin.json` and a `README`. The plan has. Fine.

**OAuth CIMD**: `@better-auth/cimd` may not exist. The plan's spike 0a. Fine.

**Token scope `spend`**: If a user grants `read` only, the agent can still call `export_documents` (write) and get signed URLs; that's a write. Fine.

**OAuth consent page**: The plan says `/oauth/consent`; Better Auth's OAuth provider may provide a consent page. Fine.

**Revocation table**: The plan says "The verifier also checks a revocation table, so an access token dies within a minute." If the table is D1 and the token is a JWT, the verifier must check on every request; a D1 read adds latency. Fine. But **the revocation table must be populated on revoke**; Better Auth's revocation may delete the refresh token but not the access token. The plan adds. Fine.

**Account deletion revokes everything**: The plan says. But the `agent_operations` and exports must be deleted on account deletion. The plan says exports deleted on account deletion; `agent_operations` has `user_id` FK cascade. Fine.

**R2 exports retention**: 30 days, cron cleanup. Existing cron has `0 3 * * *`; add cleanup. Fine.

**PostHog analytics**: `mcp_tool_called` with tool, client, duration, outcome; no content. But **client** identification may include user agent / IP; fine. The plan says "MCP analytics" in privacy policy. Fine.

**Rate limiting**: The plan mentions a daily spend cap but no **per-tool rate limit**. Add.

**Abuse of `save_cv`**: The plan says "save_cv is free" and "parses it" — LLM cost. A looping agent can overwrite the user's primary CV repeatedly. Destructive? It says "yes, confirm when replacing". But `confirm: true` is not a security boundary (as above). A prompt-injected agent can replace the user's CV with a malicious one. High.

**`update_cv_content`**: "Field-level edits to the tailored CV (`revision` required)" — no `confirm`, but it can overwrite the user's CV content. The tool is marked `destructive: no`. If it changes the summary or bullets, that's a content overwrite, but reversible via revision? The plan says no confirm. The existing chat tools route prose changes through `propose_cv_rewrite` with anti-hallucination. `update_cv_content` could be used to inject arbitrary text into the CV, bypassing the writer's anti-hallucination rules. The plan says "Field-level edits" but doesn't say it validates against the source CV or routes prose through the writer. This is a **content integrity/anti-hallucination** hole: an agent can set `summary` to any text, including fabricated claims, and the CV will export it. The web app's manual editor allows the user to type anything (user-authored), but an agent is not the user. The plan should restrict `update_cv_content` to mechanical fields (dates, titles, contact) or route prose through `rewrite_cv_section` with source verification. High. This is a strong finding for "same quality" and "anti-hallucination". The plan's `update_cv_content` is a free write, no scope `spend`, no writer. A prompt-injected agent could add a fake degree. Add.

**`edit_document_text`**: "Directly edit cover letter, email or follow-up text" — same issue: bypasses the writer/anti-hallucination. It is marked destructive (overwrites) but no confirm? "yes (overwrites)". The agent can rewrite the cover letter with fabricated claims. The human can edit text manually, but an agent should not. The plan needs a guard: only allow edits that are substring/format changes, or route through the writer with source checks. High.

**`update_application`**: Can change company address/contact person, which feeds the cover letter recipient. A prompt-injected agent could change the recipient to a fake address or a different company. Not destructive? It overwrites. Medium. The plan marks no destructive. The human can edit. Fine.

**`delete_cv_item`**: destructive; no confirm? The plan says `delete_cv_item` destructive, `revision` required, but not `confirm`. The table says "Destructive: yes" and the description doesn't mention confirm. It should require confirm, but again confirm is weak. The `delete_application` and `delete_cv` have confirm. Inconsistent. Medium.

**`rewrite_cv_section`**: "0.5, free if refused" and "Destructive: yes (overwrites)". It uses the writer with anti-hallucination. Good. But "free if refused" means a refusal costs 0; how does the agent know? Fine.

**`regenerate_document`**: "with an instruction" — the instruction is agent-authored, can be prompt-injected. The existing route caps at 300 chars and flattens. The plan should reuse that. It says "with an instruction" but not the cap. Low/medium.

**`apply_to_feed_job`**: "0.5 or 2.0" and "Feed job to application, then fit or kit". If it creates an application and runs fit, it may double-charge if the agent then calls `generate_application_kit` with a new key. The fit-continuation discount must apply. Fine.

**`draft_screening_answers`**: "Plus/Max screening answers, same turn cap as the web app". The web app's screening mode is a chat endpoint with a 5-turn cap. The MCP tool must increment `screening_turns_used` atomically and not allow the agent to reset it. The plan says "same turn cap". Fine. But the tool is in the "spend" table with "per current rules" — screening answers are included in Plus/Max, not credit-charged. The table's "Credits" column says "per current rules", which is vague. The `get_account` should show the cap. Fine.

**`export_documents`**: "any `templateId` and an optional `paper` override; returns signed links". The tool can export a **cover letter** with a `templateId`, which is meaningless. Also it can export a CV with a template that doesn't match the application's selected template, which may be desired. Fine. But the **paper override** issue and **locale** issue. Add.

**`get_operation`**: "Status and result of a queued generation". It should return the result (kit) or a link. The plan says `result_ref`. Fine.

**`cancel_generation`**: "Stop a queued or running generation (unspent credits are released)". If the generation is running, the model call can't be cancelled; the consumer must check a cancel flag before persisting. The plan doesn't say. Add to the cancel race finding.

**`set_cv_template`**: "Choose a template for an application". The existing chat tool also saves it as the user's default. The plan doesn't say whether it updates the default. Minor.

**`update_application_status`**: "Status, interview date, follow-up date (these drive the reminder emails)". The existing `setTrackerStatusAction` has specific validation (e.g., interview date required for interview, follow-up date). The plan should reuse. Fine.

**`delete_application`**: "Remove an application". The existing `deleteSessionAction` deletes the row and chat; R2 files? Fine.

**`cancel_generation`**: free, no confirm. Fine.

**`give_job_feedback`**: "Save, unsave, dismiss, rate". The existing feedback API may have different actions. Fine.

**`update_feed_settings`**: "Roles, countries, remote, salary floor, automation, timezone". The existing settings API may have more (e.g., exclude companies, seniority). Fine.

**Missing: `get_job_feed` item details / `get_feed_job`**: The agent can list the feed, but to apply to a feed job it needs the job description. `apply_to_feed_job` takes a job id. Fine.

**Missing: `search_jobs` / external job search**: The human uses the job feed, not a general search. Fine.

**Missing: `get_company_research`**: `get_application` includes company research? It says "Job, fit, status, dates, generation and operation status, kit, revision". Not company research. The human can see company research in the app. Minor.

**Missing: `refresh_company_research`**: The app may have a company research step. `update_application` sets company address/contact. Fine.

**Missing: `generate_application_email` separately**: `regenerate_document` covers email, but only if it exists. The kit generates it. Fine.

**Missing: `generate_follow_up`**: `regenerate_document` covers follow-up, but the tracker follow-up route may require an existing application. Fine.

**Missing: `get_follow_up`**: `get_application` includes kit/follow-up? It says "kit". Fine.

**Missing: `submit_feedback`**: minor.

**Missing: `get_invoices` / billing**: left out.

**Missing: `export_screening_answers`**: concrete.

**Missing: `save_screening_answers`**: The web app stores screening answers inside `generated_cv` (`_screeningAnswers`). The MCP `draft_screening_answers` should persist them; the plan says "same turn cap", but does it persist? It should. If not, the agent gets an answer but the web app doesn't show it. The plan says "persists its own result" per service. Fine.

**Missing: `update_cv_visibility`**: The plan says "Anything the user excludes is an explicit, visible choice" but no tool. Maybe the canvas has visibility toggles. Search.
dsh: reasoning:
No visibility toggle. So "Anything the user excludes is an explicit, visible choice" means the user must delete items, which is destructive. The plan removes silent truncation but doesn't add a non-destructive "hide" mechanism. A user with 20 skills who wants a 2-page CV must delete skills (and lose them from the master). The plan says "Anything the user excludes is an explicit, visible choice" but there is no exclude control. The only choice is delete (destructive) or leave all (overflow). This is a **product gap**: to remove the caps and keep layouts fitting, you need per-item visibility/exclusion (or automatic pagination). The plan says layouts wrap to fit every skill, so maybe all skills fit, but a 2-page limit means overflow. The plan's "minimum body size 9.5pt" and no caps will make CVs longer. A visibility control is needed. Add to "Missing from the plan" (high).

**`render-caps.ts` removal**: If caps are removed, `render-caps.ts` and `page_warning` disappear. The plan says "Anything the user excludes is an explicit, visible choice." But no exclude feature. Good.

**Photo / US**: The plan says "Per template, as today. `list_cv_templates` and the skill state the US norm (no photo). No silent change for existing users." This is a deliberate decision from the reviews. Is it still a defect? The repo's `COMPLIANCE_CHECKLIST.US.forbidden` includes photo, but `premium-ats` supports it. The plan says no silent change, but the product goal "best-in-class ATS" means a US CV with a photo is not best-in-class. The plan could default the photo off for US templates in the export while leaving the canvas as-is, with a warning. But that changes the canvas/PDF parity. I'd flag it as a residual high product risk, not critical.

**DACH photo**: The plan says "Optional, top right, Bewerbungsfoto ratio". Current GermanATS uses 35x47mm; GermanClassic? Let me check.
dsh: reasoning:
Same ratio. Fine.

**DIN cover letter date format**: The plan says "Berlin, 20. Oktober 2026". DIN 5008 date formats include "20. Oktober 2026" and "20.10.2026". Fine. But the plan's product rule says date ranges use a hyphen, not en dash. The German letter date doesn't use a dash. Fine.

**US cover letter date**: The plan says "US business letter, block format" but doesn't specify the date format or whether to include the sender's address at the top. The structured contract has sender, recipient, date, subject, salutation, paragraphs, closing. US block format typically includes sender address, date, recipient, salutation, body, closing, signature. The plan's structure covers. Fine.

**Cover letter "subject" in US**: US business letters often omit a subject line. The structured contract has a subject; for US, it may be optional. Fine.

**DIN "Betreff"**: The plan says subject bold, two blank lines below the address block, no "Betreff:". Correct. But it doesn't say the subject should include the job title and reference number; content issue.

**DIN "Anrede"**: The plan says "One blank line after 'Sehr geehrte …'." Correct. But the salutation should be followed by a comma and the body starts lowercase. The plan doesn't specify. Low.

**DIN "Mit freundlichen Grüßen" has no comma.** Correct. But the plan says "closing" and "signature"; the signature should be handwritten/blank space, and the name printed below. The plan's structured contract has `signatureName`; fine.

**DIN fold marks**: The plan omits. A DIN 5008 letter should include fold marks (Falzmarken) and a punch mark (Lochmarke), especially for a printed letter. For a PDF application, many German employers expect them. The plan claims "DIN 5008:2020 Form B" but omits these. Add to the DIN finding or a separate low. This is a completeness issue. Also the **Informationsblock** (sender's phone/email) at 125mm from left, max 75mm wide, is part of DIN 5008 and missing from the plan's measurements. High/medium.

**DIN address field 20mm from left vs 25mm text**: The plan says "starting 45 mm from the top and 20 mm from the left"; good. But the **return line** should be in the top line of the 5-line Zusatz-/Vermerkzone, not "top 5mm". The plan's wording is ambiguous/wrong. Covered.

**DIN Form B vs Form A**: The plan says Form B. Form B is the more common business letter. Good.

**DIN 5008:2020**: The standard is DIN 5008:2020-03. The plan says "DIN 5008:2020 Form B". Fine.

**ATS "standard headings"**: The plan says English headings "Summary, Experience, Education, Skills, Certifications, Languages." The repo's `DEFAULT_HEADERS` use "PROFESSIONAL SUMMARY", "PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE", "CORE COMPETENCIES". The plan says standard headings, but the templates' defaults differ. The plan must update the template defaults to match, or ATS parsers may not recognize "CORE COMPETENCIES" as "Skills". The plan says "Standard headings" but doesn't say the templates' defaults change. The ATS eval would catch if it compares to the source, but it might not check the exact heading names against a standard list. This is a concrete gap: `GermanATSTemplate.DEFAULT_HEADERS.skills = 'CORE COMPETENCIES'` (English) and the AI's `sectionHeaders` can be anything; the plan's "standard headings" list is not enforced. Add finding (medium). Also the German template's `DEFAULT_HEADERS` are English; if the AI doesn't provide German headers, a German CV gets English headings. The plan says "German: Profil, Berufserfahrung..." but the template defaults are English. The `localizedStrings` only covers labels, not headers. The writer prompt may set German headers. The plan should enforce/normalize by language. Good.

**Date ranges**: The plan says hyphen, never en dash. Current templates use en dash (`–`) in GermanATS line 395 and likely US templates. The plan must change all templates. Fine. But the **date formats** in `data` may already contain en dashes (AI output). The plan needs a normalization step, not just template text. The eval checks. Low.

**"No text in images"**: The current `Photo` is an image; that's allowed (photo). The plan says no text in images. Fine.

**"File size under 2 MB"**: The plan's target. Fine.

**"Contact details in body, never header/footer"**: The templates use `<header>` element, which is body content; PDF page headers/footers are separate. Fine.

**"Real text with Unicode maps"**: Chromium PDFs embed fonts with ToUnicode. But **Arimo/Gelasio base64 fonts** may be subset and embed a ToUnicode map; Chromium does. Fine. **Umlauts**: If the HTML lacks `<meta charset="utf-8">`, the text could be garbled. The docs example includes it. Add a low/medium finding: the plan doesn't specify the HTML charset; with `html` passed as a JSON string, Chromium may default to windows-1252 and mangle umlauts. The eval would catch it, but the spike should include umlauts. This is a concrete verification gap. Add low.

**Browser Run `html` + `<base>`**: Photos/fonts inlined, no subrequests. Fine. But **CSS `url()` for the photo** must be data URI; plan says. Fine.

**Browser Run `tagged`**: The plan says "tagged is on, but it's treated as accessibility, not a parsing guarantee." Good. But **pdf-lib post-processing may strip the structure tree**. As noted, add a low finding: the spike must test tagging after pdf-lib, not before. The plan's spike 0b says "tagging"; the renderer says "pdf-lib then sets metadata". If the spike tests the raw PDF, it won't catch pdf-lib stripping. Low.

**PDF metadata Language**: pdf-lib `setLanguage` exists. Good. But **`dc:language` vs `/Lang`**: pdf-lib sets `/Lang`; XMP metadata may not include `dc:language`. ATS doesn't care. Fine.

**`title`/`author`**: PDF metadata from user content; no PII leak? It's the user's own name. Fine.

**`Producer`**: "Useful Apply" or pdf-lib; fine.

**DOCX**:
- `docx` package version 9.5.1. `ImageRun` requires `type` in newer versions; fine.
- **Heading styles**: The plan says "Native paragraphs and heading styles." The `docx` `HeadingLevel` styles are built-in; fine.
- **ATS**: DOCX with headings is good. But **DOCX generated from the same structure** may include a photo (`ImageRun`) on DACH templates. ATS parsers often ignore images; fine.
- **Page size**: `docx` sections default to Letter? The plan says A4/Letter. Fine.
- **DIN cover letter DOCX**: The plan says "DOCX is generated with docx from the same structure" but doesn't specify DIN measurements. It will be a generic document, not DIN 5008. If the German cover letter DOCX is expected to be DIN-compliant, the plan under-specifies. Add to the DIN finding or a low.

**Eval**:
- **Fixtures**: "a CV that goes past every old skill cap" — good.
- "a single entry longer than one page" — good.
- "one US and one DIN 5008 cover letter" — good.
- **PDF checks**: "Full text extracted with pdftotext and pdf.js matches the source CV data: every skill, bullet, job title, date-to-employer pairing and heading, in reading order, with umlauts intact." Good. But **pdftotext and pdf.js may produce different orders**; the plan says both must match. Which is authoritative? If pdftotext uses `-layout`, it preserves visual columns; pdf.js uses content order. The plan should specify flags and expected order. Minor.
- **"Date-to-employer pairing"**: Good check for two-column.
- **"Page size, margins"**: Good. But the **DIN field positions** check must use the correct internal zones (see finding).
- **"No photo where the template has none"** — good.
- **"DOCX checks: XML content and order, plus a LibreOffice render to PDF that is then text-checked."** Good. But LibreOffice rendering may not match Word; fine.
- **"ATS-parser proxy: OpenResume"** — as noted, English-centric.
- **"Baseline: same checks on today's html2canvas export."** The baseline will fail text extraction (image), which proves the improvement. Good.
- **"Visual parity: canvas screenshot against PDF page images, judged by a clean Opus judge plus Wasim."** Good, but "clean" judge only sees screenshots; it may not catch text order. Fine.
- **"Real ATS, manual, once per market: upload to a Greenhouse or Personio test posting"** — good, but "Personio test posting" may not exist; Wasim provides accounts. Fine.
- **"Environment: `wrangler dev --remote`"** — as noted, Browser Run can run locally with `remote: true`; `wrangler dev --remote` may not be compatible with OpenNext. Low.
- **MCP E2E**:
  - "OAuth with consent" — good.
  - "Upload a German CV and run a fit check on a senior job description." Good.
  - "Generate the kit and poll it." Good.
  - "Edit a CV field, delete an item and rewrite a section." Good.
  - "Regenerate the cover letter." Good.
  - "Export all five formats in two templates." Good.
  - "Set the status to interview with a date." Good.
  - "Check that /dashboard shows the same kit and tracker state." Good.
  - **Negative paths**: another user's ids, read token, expired/revoked token, same key twice, same key different input, Queue crash, stale tab, hostile JD, daily cap. Good. Missing:
    - **Prompt-injected destructive tool** (hostile JD tries `delete_application` with confirm) — the plan says "tries to trigger save_cv or delete_application" but expects the model to refuse. The test should assert the server rejects destructive actions without a real user confirmation; if the model can be tricked, the test may fail. Good, but the plan doesn't say how the test simulates a compromised model. It says "hostile job description that tries to trigger save_cv or delete_application". If the MCP tool requires `confirm: true` and the hostile JD says "confirm: true", the model may call it. The test would expose the weakness. Good.
    - **Double charge due to client retry with a new key** — not tested. The plan tests same key twice, not fresh key. Add.
    - **Lease expiry / duplicate consumer** — the plan tests "Queue crash mid-run", not lease expiry/redelivery. Add.
    - **Partial kit failure** — not tested. Add.
    - **Cancel vs completion race** — not tested. Add.
    - **Browser Run failure fallback** — not tested. Add.
    - **Export signed URL expiry/IDOR** — not tested. Add.
    - **Oversized upload** — not tested. Add.
    - **Rate limit** — not tested. Add.
  - **Live checks**: Claude.ai custom connector, Claude Code, MCP Inspector, ChatGPT developer mode. Good. Missing: **Cursor/Codex**? The plan says any other MCP client; not necessary. **ChatGPT directory review** live check is later.
- **Verification of the "same renderer"**: The plan says "canvas screenshot against PDF page images" but not **pixel diff** or **text diff**. A human judge may miss. Fine.

**PR ordering**:
- "PR 0a/0b. Spikes (no merge)" but then "findings committed to docs/agent-integration/spikes.md". If they don't merge, how are findings committed? A commit on a branch? The plan says no merge, but the findings need to be on `main` for the next PRs. Minor.
- **PR 2 starts now, parallel with 1.** PR 2 includes `agent_operations` and revision columns; PR 1 includes OAuth migration. Both touch `migrations/d1/` (shared file). The plan says "the orchestrator alone edits migrations", so they can't be parallel unless the orchestrator serializes migrations. The plan says "Shared files: the orchestrator alone edits ... migrations". Fine, but the PRs still conflict; the orchestrator must manage. Low.
- **PR 3 after spike 0b, but PR 3's renderer needs the `BROWSER` binding in `wrangler.jsonc`, which is a shared file.** The orchestrator handles. Fine.
- **PR 5 after 1 and 2, but PR 3's template changes (removing caps) break `cv-tools.ts` (PR 5)**. Add.
- **PR 6 after 3,4,5.** Good.
- **PR 4 structured cover letter starts now, but PR 3's cover letter layouts depend on it.** Add.

**Simpler alternatives**:
- **Cloudflare Workflows** for generation: durable, retries, no manual lease/reconcile. But the plan's Queue + leases is a proven pattern in the repo. Maybe not simpler.
- **Durable Object per user** for serialization: avoids revision conflicts, but D1 is the source of truth. No.
- **Use the existing `generated_artifacts` column** instead of a new `agent_operations` table for generation status? `agent_operations` is needed for idempotency/spend. Fine.
- **Use a single `documents` service** with a `revision` column on `application_sessions`; fine.
- **Skip the R2 export cache** initially; generate on demand. But signed URLs and caching are useful. The cache adds complexity (HMAC, cleanup). For a first release, generating on each export (3s) is simpler and avoids stale-cache/ownership bugs. The plan's R2 cache is not strictly necessary. Suggest as a simplification: return the PDF bytes via MCP resource or a short-lived signed URL; cache only if needed. But MCP tool output can't return binary; signed URL is needed. R2 storage is the simplest. Fine.
- **Use a single `export_documents` tool that returns a ZIP** instead of five links? MCP clients may not handle ZIP. Fine.
- **Use `pdf-lib` to generate the PDF directly**? No.
- **Use one ATS template for all exports**? Simpler and better ATS, but loses template choice. Could offer "ATS-safe" variants. Mention.

**Directory review rejection risks**:
- **40 tools + 13 read + 6 spend + 20 free writes**: The plan says 40, but actual table is 39. A directory reviewer may not care, but the submission pack will be wrong. Low.
- **Tool annotations**: `get_account` includes plan/balance; `readOnlyHint: true` is correct. `export_documents` creates files but is read-only? It's marked no destructive, free write. It has side effects (R2 writes), so `readOnlyHint: false`; the plan's read/write separation puts it in "Write, free", so false. Good.
- **`run_job_feed`** has external side effects (fetches jobs, LLM) but is marked no destructive. `openWorldHint: true`. Fine.
- **`save_cv`** is destructive when replacing; `destructiveHint: true` only "when replacing". Tool annotations are static, not conditional. The plan says "yes, `confirm` when replacing" — but `destructiveHint` is a static boolean; it can't be "when replacing". The table's "Destructive" column says "yes, confirm when replacing", which is not a valid annotation. This is a directory-review risk: annotations must be static booleans. The plan's "Every tool sets ... explicit booleans" but the table uses conditional values. Add finding (medium). Similarly `generate_application_kit` "yes when regenerate", `regenerate_document` "yes (overwrites)", `rewrite_cv_section` "yes (overwrites)". If the tool can be destructive, set `destructiveHint: true` always. The plan should fix the table and the annotations. Good.
- **`delete_cv_item`** destructive but no confirm; `delete_application` confirm. Inconsistent.
- **`export_documents`** returns signed links; `openWorldHint: false` (it doesn't access external world), fine.
- **`get_job_feed`** openWorld true; fine.
- **`analyze_job_fit`** creates a draft; `readOnlyHint: false`, `destructiveHint: false`. Fine.
- **`draft_screening_answers`** "per current rules" — the tool description must state the plan requirement (Plus/Max) and the cap. Fine.
- **Commerce**: `get_account` returns "plan" and "balance"; ChatGPT may consider this "selling" if it displays plan names. The plan says no upgrade text. Fine.
- **OAuth CIMD/DCR**: OpenAI may require DCR; Anthropic may require CIMD or DCR. The plan has both. Good.
- **Reviewer account**: as noted, needs email verification, credits, CV, feed consent, no MFA. The plan says "seeded reviewer account (no MFA)" but not the rest. Add.
- **Privacy policy**: must cover **Browser Run processing** (the plan says), **MCP analytics** (plan says), **R2 exports** (plan says), **OAuth tokens** (not mentioned), **subprocessors** (Cloudflare, Anthropic, OpenAI? The MCP server calls Anthropic; the privacy policy must list Anthropic as a subprocessor). The plan says "privacy and terms updates" but not the specific subprocessors. Low/medium.
- **Data deletion**: The plan leaves out account deletion; the privacy policy must provide a deletion path. The web app has `/api/auth/delete-account`; the reviewer can use it. Fine.
- **Terms**: ChatGPT app directory requires terms; plan says.
- **Support URL**: plan says.
- **Logo/screenshots**: plan says "I prepare every field, the example prompts, the screenshots". Good.
- **Anthropic connector directory**: requires the server to be **publicly reachable** and **OAuth**. Fine.
- **OpenAI domain challenge**: plan says. Fine.
- **OpenAI app manifest**: The plan says "OpenAI manifest"; current OpenAI apps use an `ai-plugin.json`? If the manifest format is wrong, review rejection. The plan should name the exact manifest/verification file and current spec. Low.

**Security: OAuth**
- **Redirect URI allowlist**: exact match for Claude, ChatGPT, loopback. Good. But **loopback** redirect URIs (Claude Code, Cursor) must allow arbitrary ports (`http://127.0.0.1:{port}/callback`) per RFC 8252; exact-match allowlist can't enumerate ports. The plan says "exact-match allowlist for Claude, ChatGPT and loopback (Claude Code, Cursor)". If exact match includes a fixed port, Claude Code/Cursor may use a random port and fail. The correct approach for loopback is to allow `http://127.0.0.1`/`http://localhost` with any port, or use the client's registered redirect. This is a concrete OAuth bug. Add finding (medium). The plan's "exact-match" is good for web clients but wrong for loopback. Claude Code's OAuth callback uses a fixed port? I think Claude Code uses `http://localhost:PORT/callback` with a random port. Cursor similar. So exact match breaks them. Good.
- **PKCE S256 required**: good.
- **Resource parameter bound**: good.
- **CIMD fetch caps**: good.
- **DCR**: DCR allows anyone to register a client; rate-limit/abuse. The plan says DCR stays on. DCR can be abused to create many clients; need rate limiting and cleanup. Low.
- **Token audience**: The plan says "The resource parameter is bound" but doesn't say the JWT `aud` is checked against `https://usefulapply.com/mcp`. If not, a token from another resource could be replayed. The plan says "resource parameter is bound", likely covers. Fine.
- **Token scope**: The verifier checks scopes; good.
- **Revocation**: good.
- **Refresh token rotation**: good.
- **Account deletion revokes**: good.
- **Consent page CSRF**: The plan says `/oauth/consent`; no CSRF mention. Better Auth likely handles. Low.
- **OAuth client secrets**: Public clients (Claude/ChatGPT) use PKCE, no secret. Fine.
- **CIMD**: Client ID Metadata Document URLs can be SSRF; the plan caps size/time but not allowed schemes/hosts. A malicious CIMD URL could target internal metadata. The plan says "CIMD fetches are capped for size and time" but not **SSRF allowlist** (https only, public IPs, no redirects). This is a security hole. Add finding (medium). The global fetch flag `global_fetch_strictly_public` in wrangler.jsonc helps (it blocks private IPs), but the plan should rely on it/mention. Actually `global_fetch_strictly_public` is set, so `fetch` to private IPs is blocked. Good, but redirects? The flag blocks. Dismiss or mention.
- **DCR open registration**: spam. Low.

**Security: IDOR**
- Services check ownership in query. But `export_documents` takes `applicationId` and `templateId`; the R2 key includes userId. Fine.
- `get_operation` takes `operationId`; check user. Plan says.
- `cancel_generation` takes `operationId`; check user.
- `save_cv` writes to the user's own documents. Fine.
- `set_profile_photo` writes to the user's own R2. Fine.
- **`get_cv` "Primary, master or tailored CV"** — the tailored CV requires an application ID; check ownership. Fine.

**Security: prompt injection through job descriptions or tool output**
- The plan wraps output; but the **writer prompt** consumes the JD. The existing `sanitizeAdText` in chat tools. The plan says "the untrusted-content wrapper in chat-tools/untrusted.ts" is ready to reuse. Fine.
- **Tool output** includes user CV content and job descriptions; the agent may be tricked. The plan says "Tool results contain facts only, never instructions." Fine.
- **Destructive tools with `confirm: true`** — the main hole. High.
- **`edit_document_text` / `update_cv_content`** — content injection. High.

**Rate-limit / credit abuse by a looping agent**
- Daily cap 10 credits, 2 concurrent. But free LLM `save_cv` and free Browser Run exports. High.
- **Read tools** can be looped; no per-minute rate limit. Medium.
- **`draft_screening_answers`** may be free (Plus/Max) and capped per application; a looping agent can create applications and use 5 turns each. But creating applications is free; the daily cap only counts credits. If screening answers cost no credits, the agent can generate unlimited screening answers by creating applications. The web app's screening cap is per application, not per user/day. A looping agent could create 100 applications and get 500 screening answers, each an LLM call. The plan says "same turn cap as the web app" but no global cap. This is a cost abuse hole. Add to the free LLM finding.
- **`run_job_feed`** one/day, fine.
- **`export_documents`** free, unlimited. High.

**Credit and idempotency bugs**
- Fresh-key retries. High.
- Success path not atomic. High.
- Partial kit refunds. Medium.
- Cancel race. High.
- Lease expiry/duplicate. High.
- Reservation key global unique. Low.
- Daily cap adjustable. Low.

**Workers limits**
- Queue consumer wall time. Fine.
- Browser Run 30 rps. Fine.
- CPU 300s. Fine.
- **D1 batch size**: A batch with `persistKit` (large JSON) + capture + ledger may exceed D1's 100 KB per statement? D1 max SQL statement length 100 KB? D1 has a 1 MB max row size; a generated CV JSON can be large (with chat?), but `persistKit` writes `generated_cv` and `cover_letter`; could be >100 KB. Existing code writes the same. Fine.
- **D1 `batch()` max statements**: 100? Fine.
- **Queue message size**: only op id. Fine.

**Export fidelity**
- Fixed-width templates vs paper override. High.
- Two-column text order. High.
- CSS/padding double margins. Medium.
- 9.5pt floor vs 11-12px. Medium.
- Browser raster fallback. High.
- Photo on US template. Medium.
- Sidebar doesn't repeat content. Medium.
- Fonts: Arimo/Gelasio self-hosted; good. But **Gelasio** is not metric-compatible with Georgia? Let me verify. Gelasio is "metric-compatible with Georgia" per Google Fonts. Yes.
- **Arial vs Arimo**: Arimo is metric-compatible with Arial. Good.
- **Ligatures off**: `font-variant-ligatures: none`; Chromium supports. ATS text extraction of "fi" ligatures: Chromium ToUnicode maps ligatures to "fi"; but turning off is safer. Good.
- **oklch**: Chromium supports; the plan doesn't mention, but the browser fallback strips oklch (existing code). If the server renderer uses the template's inline colors (hex), fine. If it includes Tailwind classes (`bg-white` -> oklch), Chromium handles. Dismiss.
- **Photos in PDF**: Base64 data URI; Chromium loads. Good.
- **A4 vs Letter**: fixed width. High.
- **Page breaks**: CSS `break-inside: avoid` on flex containers may not work. Medium.
- **Two-column page 2**: sidebar content not repeated; the plan only says background repeats. Medium.
- **Tagged PDF**: spike. Low.
- **PDF text order**: high.
- **Cover letter DIN**: high.

**Directory review rejection risks**:
- 39 vs 40 tools. Low.
- Conditional destructive annotations. Medium.
- Reviewer account not fully seeded. Medium.
- Loopback exact-match. Medium.
- No `WWW-Authenticate` 401. Medium.
- **OAuth consent screen**: The plan says `/oauth/consent`, but Anthropic's connector directory requires the OAuth flow to show **which scopes** and **which app**; fine.
- **CIMD**: OpenAI's directory may not support CIMD; DCR stays on. Fine.
- **Commerce**: credit-metered product; no checkout. Fine.
- **Data retention**: exports 30 days; privacy policy. Fine.
- **Tool descriptions**: "one or two sentences" — the existing chat tool descriptions are long (several paragraphs) because they encode rules. The plan says each description stays one or two sentences. For 40 tools, one or two sentences may be insufficient for the model to use them correctly (especially the CV edit tools with gates). The plan's own catalog has complex tools (`edit_cv_structure`, `generate_application_kit`) that need detailed descriptions. The "one or two sentences" constraint may hurt quality. Directory reviewers prefer concise descriptions, but tool selection accuracy matters. Low/medium. Could mention as a tradeoff, not a defect.
- **Tool count vs model context**: 40 tools in every request; OpenAI/Claude tool limits? Claude supports many tools; ChatGPT may have a limit (128). Fine.
- **`title`**: The plan says every tool sets `title`; MCP tool `title` is optional but directory may require. Fine.

**Factual claims against code**:
- **Tool count**: 39, not 40. Low.
- **"All 6 templates silently cut skills to 5 per group"**: verified.
- **"render-caps.ts"** is not the cause; the `.slice()` calls are. Low.
- **"The cover letter PDF is plain Helvetica"**: verified.
- **"The CV PDF is an image of the page"**: verified (JPEG).
- **"The browser owns the kit ... every 2 seconds"**: verified.
- **"A headless kit never fills canonical columns"**: verified for agent-v2; regenerate/follow-up do. The claim is about kit generation. Fine.
- **"Key actions are server actions only"**: `update_application_status` has `setTrackerStatusAction`; but there are also API routes for tracker? `src/app/api/tracker/*` includes follow-up, maybe status. Let me check.
dsh: reasoning:
Tracker status is a server action. Fine.

- **"Credit reservations with idempotency keys"** verified.
- **"The feed's leased generation runner ... reuse"** verified pattern, but it's not a Queue; it's an SSE/lease. The plan says "ready to reuse" but the actual feed runner uses `lease_owner` on a row, not a Queue consumer. The plan's claim is okay as a pattern. But the feed runner's lease logic might have bugs; let me read `src/lib/job-feed/server.ts` around 430-470 to see.
dsh: reasoning:
The feed runner doesn't have a conditional claim (`WHERE status=...`); it reads then updates. The plan says "lease claim by conditional update", which is better. Fine.

**"The chat tools' read handlers"** ready to reuse. Some read handlers are tied to `ctx` and client session; the services need refactoring. Fine.

**"The untrusted-content wrapper"** ready. Fine.

**"There's no public print route"** - the plan's architecture says no public print route. Good.

**"No render token"** - good.

**"Browser Run Quick Actions bill browser time only"** - verified.

**"Workers Paid includes 10 hours a month"** - verified.

**"Queues are within included operations"** - Workers Paid Queues pricing: 1 million operations/month included? At our volume, fine.

**"Expected incremental bill: $0"** - Browser Run 10 hours included; 12,000 PDFs at 3s = 10 hours. The plan says "about $0.000075 a PDF beyond roughly 12,000 a month", so $0 at <12,000. Fine. But **Cloudflare R2 storage/egress** for exports: R2 has 10 GB free, then $0.015/GB-month; egress free. 30-day retention, 5 formats x 6 templates. At scale, small. Fine.

**"Queue-based generation"** - Cloudflare Queues requires a paid plan; Workers Paid. Fine.

**"D1 with `transaction: false`"** - Better Auth D1 adapter. Fine.

**"@better-auth/mcp"** - need to verify package. Let me check npm? No network maybe. Use web search.
dsh: reasoning:
The packages exist. Good. Dismiss.

Let me read the Better Auth MCP/CIMD docs for OAuth details that could contradict the plan (e.g., DCR, scopes, resource).
dsh: reasoning:
Very useful. Key facts:
- MCP plugin has `requireMcpAuth` that already handles 401/WWW-Authenticate and scope challenges. The plan's "per-tool scope check" can use `createInsufficientScopeError`. Fine.
- **DCR is deprecated by MCP; Better Auth never enables DCR implicitly.** The plan says "CIMD is advertised and DCR stays on." That's a deliberate choice; DCR can be enabled explicitly. Fine.
- **CIMD on Workers requires a custom transport** that resolves DNS, pins, rejects private IPs/redirects. The plan says "CIMD fetches are capped for size and time" but does **not** mention the required secure transport. On Workers, `@better-auth/cimd/node` won't work; they need a custom transport. The plan's spike 0a says "Confirm exact versions, the OAuth tables on D1 with transaction: false, and the /oauth2/* routing under OpenNext." It doesn't mention the CIMD Workers transport. This is a concrete blocker: CIMD on Workers cannot use the bundled Node transport; the plan must implement a DNS-pinned transport or accept the SSRF risk. The docs are explicit: "Bun, Deno, Workers, and other runtimes must provide the equivalent secure transport at their network boundary." The plan's "CIMD fetches are capped for size and time" is insufficient. This is a **critical/high** finding. Also `global_fetch_strictly_public` may block private IPs but does not pin the address or refuse redirects (the docs say do not DNS-check then call globalThis.fetch). So the plan needs a custom transport. Great catch.

- **MCP 2026-07-28 requires `legacy: "reject"`** in `createMcpHandler`; the plan says "Accept legacy stateless MCP clients as well as the 2026-07-28 spec." Better Auth docs say configure servers with `legacy: "reject"` and accept only 2026-07-28; the plan's decision 6 says accept legacy too. That's a deliberate deviation. Is it supported? The SDK's `createMcpHandler` option `legacy: "reject"` means reject legacy; to accept legacy, use a different setting. The plan says "Accepts legacy stateless clients as well as the 2026-07-28 spec." This may conflict with the Better Auth MCP plugin's expectations. Not necessarily a defect; the SDK supports both. But directory review may prefer current spec. Low.

- **`requireMcpAuth` checks access token locally against JWKS, no DB round trip.** The plan says "The verifier also checks a revocation table, so an access token dies within a minute." That adds a DB round trip; fine. But Better Auth's `requireMcpAuth` doesn't check a revocation table; the plan would wrap/augment. Fine.

- **Scopes**: `mcp({ scopes })` config. The plan says `read`, `write`, `spend`; fine.

- **`@better-auth/mcp` requires `jwt()`**; plan says jwt(). Good.

- **`consentPage`**: `/oauth/consent`; plan says. Good.

- **`loginPage`**: `/sign-in`; plan says "OAuth on existing Better Auth login". Good.

- **MCP route must export only POST** for stateless; plan says `/mcp` Next route. Fine.

- **`createMcpHandler` from `@modelcontextprotocol/server` v2**, not Cloudflare's `agents/mcp`. The plan says "Cloudflare's stateless createMcpHandler" — that's wrong. The Better Auth docs use `createMcpHandler` from `@modelcontextprotocol/server` (official MCP TypeScript SDK v2), not Cloudflare. Cloudflare's `agents/mcp` also has `createMcpHandler`? The plan's phrasing "Cloudflare's stateless createMcpHandler" may be factually wrong. The plan also says "McpAgent is deprecated" — Cloudflare's `McpAgent` is not deprecated by Cloudflare, though the MCP spec may deprecate sessions. This is a low/medium factual issue: the plan should name the official SDK v2 `createMcpHandler`, not Cloudflare's. The architecture diagram says `/mcp (Next route, requireMcpAuth)`. Fine. I can flag as a factual error (low) or dismiss. It matters because the implementation will follow the wrong package. Add low finding.

- **CIMD metadataProfile**: `mcp-2026-07-28`; the plan says `@better-auth/cimd` but not the profile. Spike.

- **DCR fallback application type**: The docs say Cursor/older Claude Code register `localhost`/`127.0.0.1` callbacks; `clientRegistrationDefaultApplicationType: "infer"` is needed. The plan says "exact-match allowlist for Claude, ChatGPT and loopback (Claude Code, Cursor)". Better Auth's DCR with `infer` handles loopback; an exact-match allowlist would reject random ports. This reinforces the loopback finding. The plan should rely on Better Auth's native client validation and `infer`, not a hand-rolled exact-match allowlist. Good.

- **Refresh token reuse interval**: Better Auth defaults 30s for MCP clients; the plan says "Refresh tokens rotate." Fine.

- **DPoP**: Better Auth supports DPoP; the plan doesn't mention. ChatGPT/Claude may not require. Low.

- **`resource` must be HTTPS no query**; plan says. Fine.

- **CIMD loopback Client Identifier URLs not supported**; irrelevant.

**OAuth CIMD on Workers** is a strong high/critical finding. The plan's spike 0a says "CIMD on D1 + OpenNext" but not the transport. The plan says "CIMD fetches are capped for size and time" — that's the plugin's app-layer limits, not the network transport. Need to add.

**MCP SDK package**: The plan says "Cloudflare's stateless `createMcpHandler`; `McpAgent` is deprecated". Better Auth docs use `@modelcontextprotocol/server` v2. Cloudflare's `agents` package may have a `createMcpHandler` too, but the plan's wording is off. Low.

Now, let me check the **current Next.js 16 / OpenNext route** constraints for `/mcp`: Next route handlers use `runtime = 'nodejs'`; the MCP SDK v2 may require Node APIs. OpenNext Workers with `nodejs_compat` can run. Fine.

**D1 OAuth tables with `transaction: false`**: Better Auth's OAuth provider may require transactions; D1 doesn't support interactive transactions. The plan's spike. Good.

**`oauthClient` table** may have a unique index on `client_id`; CIMD URLs. Fine.

**Consent page** must be a Next page, not API. Fine.

Now let me check **AGENTS.md architecture** for any claims. It says "CV templates ... Arial body + Georgia headers (system fonts, ATS-compliant)". The plan changes to Arimo/Gelasio. Fine. It says "6 CV templates". Good.

**The plan's "Claude Code plugin ... `.mcp.json` plus one `apply-to-job` skill"**: A Claude Code plugin for a remote MCP server needs the OAuth flow; `.mcp.json` can specify `"type": "http", "url": "https://usefulapply.com/mcp"`. Fine.

**The plan's "ChatGPT plugin"**: OpenAI calls them "apps", not plugins. The plan uses "ChatGPT plugin (OpenAI directory)". The current terminology is "ChatGPT app" (Apps SDK). A directory submission under "plugin" may be wrong. This is a naming/factual issue for the OpenAI directory. Low. The plan's "OpenAI manifest" may be outdated; current Apps SDK uses an MCP server and a `chatgpt-app.json`? Let me check. The OpenAI Apps SDK docs (2025) use `app.json`? Actually the Apps SDK uses an MCP server and a `openai/outputTemplate` resource; the directory submission is via the OpenAI platform. There's no "manifest" like the old plugins. The plan's "OpenAI manifest and domain challenge" may be outdated. This is a directory-review risk: the plan should name the current Apps SDK submission artifacts. Low/medium. But I don't have a source; avoid overclaiming. I can phrase as "verify the current OpenAI Apps SDK submission format; 'plugin' and 'manifest' are the old plugin spec names."

**Claude connector directory**: Anthropic calls them "connectors" now. Fine.

**Directory tool count**: 39 not 40. Low.

**Reviewer account**: The plan says "seeded reviewer account (no MFA)". But Better Auth's `requireEmailVerification: true` means the seeded account must have `emailVerified: true`. Also the app's sign-in may require a verified email; the seed script must set it. And the reviewer needs a primary CV; `get_account` says "CV on file". Without a CV, every generation fails. The seed script must insert a CV, credits, and maybe a sample application. Add finding.

**Data isolation for reviewer**: The seed account should not be a real user. Fine.

**Privacy policy**: The plan says "privacy and terms updates" but the **MCP server processes data on Anthropic's API** (the writer) and **Cloudflare Browser Run**; the privacy policy must list these subprocessors and the data retention. The plan says "The privacy policy covers R2 exports, Browser Run processing and MCP analytics." It doesn't mention **Anthropic API** (already a subprocessor for the web app) or **OpenAI/ChatGPT** (the client). Fine.

**OpenAI commerce**: The plan says "ChatGPT forbids selling credits or plans in-app. Spending credits a user already has is fine." Is that accurate? OpenAI's app monetization policy may allow digital goods via "Instant Checkout" only for physical goods; subscriptions are not allowed. The plan avoids checkout. Fine. But **the app's core functionality is credit-metered**, and a new user with 4 free credits can generate 2 kits. The reviewer account has credits. Fine.

**Anthropic directory**: Anthropic's connector directory may require **no destructive tools without confirmation**. The plan's `confirm` is weak. Directory review might reject a `delete_application` tool that can be triggered by the model. Anthropic's tool use guidelines recommend confirmations for destructive actions. The plan's confirm flag may satisfy the letter, but not the spirit. High.

**Tool annotations for `delete_cv_item`**: destructive but no confirm. Inconsistent. Add.

**`withdraw_feed_consent`**: destructive, confirm. Running it may delete feed data; the agent could be tricked. Same confirm issue.

**`save_cv`**: replacing the primary CV is destructive; confirm weak.

**`update_profile`**: can change contact details used in CVs; not destructive but security-sensitive. A prompt-injected agent could change the user's phone/email. The human can fix. Medium.

**`set_profile_photo`**: can set a malicious image; not destructive. Fine.

**`export_documents` signed URLs**: The agent can export an application and share the link; the link is bearer. Fine.

**Render-token leakage**: The plan says no render token, good. But the **signed export URL** is a token; if the agent outputs it in a tool result, the MCP client logs it. The plan's 15-min expiry and `no-store`/`no-referrer` are good. But the **R2 object key** includes `userId` and content hash; if the URL is leaked, the recipient can download. That's the point. Fine.

**IDOR via R2 key**: A cache hit re-checks ownership; good. But the **download route** must validate the HMAC before reading R2, and the HMAC must include the key. Plan says. Fine.

**Signed URL `exp`**: 15 min; good. **Clock skew**: fine.

**Export cache invalidation**: If the user edits the CV, the content hash changes; old export remains until 30 days. Fine. If the template code changes (rendererVersion), the hash changes; good. If the **font files** change, the hash doesn't include font version; `rendererVersion` should bump manually. The plan includes `rendererVersion`; good.

**Photo hash**: If the photo URL is a signed URL that changes but the bytes are same, hash same. Fine. If the photo is removed, new hash. Good.

**`locale` in hash**: Good, but the tool doesn't take a locale; the plan says "locale" in the hash. Where does locale come from? Application's letter language? The plan doesn't define. This ties to the letter locale gap.

**Paper in hash**: Good.

**Template ID in hash**: Good.

**Content hash**: If the content is the rendered HTML, the hash includes fonts? The plan says `sha256(content, template, paper, locale, rendererVersion, photoHash)`. If `content` is the kit JSON, two different kits could render identically? Fine. If `content` is the HTML, it includes base64 fonts (huge) and the photo data URI; hashing is expensive but fine. The key length? SHA-256 hex 64 chars. R2 key length limit 1024 bytes. Fine.

**Export cleanup**: 30 days; cron. Fine.

**R2 bucket**: uses existing `FILES` bucket; exports under `exports/{userId}/...`. The existing `USER_STORAGE_QUOTA_BYTES` and `listUserFilesAction` may count exports? The plan doesn't say. If exports are stored in `FILES`, they may consume the user's storage quota or appear in file listings. Need a separate prefix and exclude from user quota/listing. Low/medium. Add to missing work.

**R2 signed URLs**: R2 supports presigned URLs via S3 API, but the plan uses an HMAC-signed app URL served through the Worker. Fine; avoids S3 creds. Good.

**`no-referrer`**: Good.

**CSP**: The download route returns PDF; no HTML. Fine.

**Content-Disposition**: The plan says file names; the download route must set `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=...` with sanitized names. Fine.

**MCP resources**: The plan returns signed links, not MCP resources. ChatGPT/Claude may not render PDFs inline; the plan says signed links are the fallback. Good.

**MCP tool output size**: `get_application` full kit can be huge (CV + letter + email + follow-up). The plan has `concise` mode. Good. But **`get_job_feed`** returns paginated job descriptions; `concise` mode. Fine. **`get_cv`** returns structured CV; could be large. The plan doesn't mention a size cap. MCP tool results have limits (Claude/ChatGPT context). A 10-page CV JSON could be 50KB; fine. A job description 100KB; capped? The plan says `concise` never returns raw JD. Good.

**MCP prompt injection through `get_job_feed`**: wrapped. Fine.

**MCP error messages**: Generic errors; the plan doesn't mention. The AGENTS security rule says generic error messages. The MCP tools should not leak stack traces/D1 errors. The plan's error codes (`error_code`) are generic. Fine.

**MCP logging**: PostHog `mcp_tool_called` with client/duration/outcome, no content. Good. But **Workers Logs** may log tool arguments (CV content) if the handler logs; the plan should say no content in logs. It says PostHog no content, not Workers Logs. Low.

**Analytics**: `mcp_tool_called` for every tool call; a looping agent generates many events. Fine.

**Rate limit binding**: Add `RATE_LIMIT_MCP` per user (e.g., 60/min) and export-specific. Good.

**Queue consumer observability**: Logs; fine.

**D1 `agent_operations` cleanup**: The table grows indefinitely. The plan doesn't mention retention/cleanup. A looping agent + retries creates rows. Add cleanup (e.g., 90 days) and index. Low/medium.

**`agent_operations` idempotency key**: Unique `(user_id, kind, idempotency_key)`. If the client reuses a key for a different `kind`, it creates a second operation; fine. If the same key with different input, reject. Good. But **the operation row is written after the credit reservation**; if the operation insert fails (unique conflict), the reservation is held. The flow should reserve and insert in one batch, or handle the conflict by releasing. The plan says "The tool reserves credits. It writes the operation..." — two steps. A crash between them leaves a reservation with no operation; reconcile must release. Fine. But a duplicate request with the same key: the second reserves (dedupes to the same reservation), then writes the operation (unique conflict) -> should return the existing operation, not error. The plan says "A retry with the same key returns the same operation." The implementation must check the operation before reserving, or handle the conflict. Fine.

**Credit reservation idempotency key**: The plan says the tool requires an idempotency key; the reservation key should be derived from the operation id/key. If the same key is used for a different tool, the reservation action could differ; the `credit_reservations` unique key is global. Fine.

**Queue message id**: The message carries only the operation id. If the operation is deleted, ack. Fine.

**Dead-letter queue**: The plan says. But **DLQ messages** need a consumer/alert; otherwise they sit. Add. Low.

**Reconcile pass**: "A reconcile pass in the existing cron repairs any stuck reservation." The cron `*/10` runs; but if the Worker is down, no. Fine. **Reconcile must not refund a generation that is still running**; it should check `lease_expires_at` and `status`. The plan says "stuck reservation" but not the criteria. Add.

**"A test kills the Worker between every step."** Good, but how? Cloudflare doesn't let you kill a Worker deterministically; they can inject a fault. Fine.

**"At most 2 concurrent generations per user"**: Need an atomic claim. D1 conditional insert/count. Add.

**"generate_application_kit refuses when the application already has a finished kit, unless regenerate: true"**: Good, prevents accidental double charge. But if the kit is partially generated/failed, it should allow. Fine. **`regenerate: true` is destructive** and the tool is marked destructive "yes when regenerate". Static annotation issue.

**`apply_to_feed_job` "0.5 or 2.0"**: If it creates an application and runs fit, then the agent calls generate with the fit discount; fine. But if it runs the kit directly (2.0), it must not also charge fit. Fine.

**"Free tier gets the full tool set, with web-app gates plus the daily MCP cap."** The web app gates some features (screening answers Plus/Max, job feed maybe free). The MCP tools must enforce the same gates. The plan says "web-app gates" but doesn't enumerate. `draft_screening_answers` says Plus/Max. `get_job_feed` free? Fine. **Free tier daily cap 10 credits**: free tier gets 4 credits/month, so the cap is above their monthly credits. Fine.

**"A per-user daily MCP spend cap (default 10 credits a day, adjustable in Connected apps)"**: If the user is on Free (4 credits/month), the cap doesn't bind; credits do. Fine.

**"Out-of-credit errors are neutral facts. They may link to an informational plans page, never to checkout."** Good for ChatGPT. But **Anthropic** may allow checkout? The plan applies to both. Fine.

**"Tool text is neutral: no 'tell the user', no upsell, no billing links."** Good. But the **Claude Code skill** can say "confirm spend with the user"; that's client-side, not tool text. Fine.

**"Read and write tools are separate. There's no generic executor"**: Good for annotations. But 40 tools is a lot; a generic `cv` tool with operations would reduce count but annotations. Fine.

**"names are 64 characters or fewer"**: fine.

**"Every tool sets all four annotations explicitly"**: The MCP SDK's `registerTool` annotations include `title`, `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `idempotentHint`, `openWorldHint`. The plan lists four, omitting `idempotentHint`. Directory reviewers may expect it; not required. But for spending tools, `idempotentHint` should be true. The plan says "all four" (title + three hints), not `idempotentHint`. Low.

**"Every spending tool states its cost and returns creditsUsed and the new balance."** Good. But **`analyze_job_fit` on a pasted JD creates a draft**; the cost is 0.5. Fine.

