## Missing from the plan
- Add CV item (experience, education, skill group/item, project, certification, language, custom section) — the catalog only edits/deletes/reorders; `cv-tools.ts:171/206/320/385`.
- Non-destructive hide/exclude of CV items/sections; removing caps without it forces deletion (`render-caps.ts:8-12`).
- `screening_answers_pdf`/`txt` in `export_documents`; the web app exports them (`AnswersPanel.tsx:48`, `KitExportHost.tsx:111`).
- Notification/settings management (`update_profile` is contact only), connected accounts, default template.
- Cover letter layout preview in the web app, so users see the DIN/US layout before download.
- MCP rate-limit bindings, `agent_operations` retention/cleanup, DLQ alerting, and an R2 export prefix excluded from the user storage quota.
- Date separator change across all six templates (all use en dash, e.g. `USPremiumATSTemplate.tsx:432`) and the DACH margin change in PR 3's work list.
- OAuth consent scope copy and a `WWW-Authenticate`/step-up test (though `requireMcpAuth` provides the headers).

## DISMISSED
- pdf-lib can set Language: `PDFDocument.d.ts:275` has `setLanguage`; the metadata claim is fine.
- Browser Run supports custom fonts via `addStyleTag`; base64 `@font-face` works ([custom fonts](https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-run/features/custom-fonts/)).
- `tagged` is an accessibility extra, not an ATS guarantee; spike 0b tests it, so the plan's caveat is sufficient.
- `@better-auth/mcp` and `@better-auth/cimd` are real packages; the plan's spike covers version/routing.
- "McpAgent is deprecated" is imprecise but not material; the stateless handler is still the right choice.
- oklch: Chromium supports CSS Color 4; the server renderer isn't html2canvas. The fallback is a separate finding.
- DIN date right-aligned, bold subject with no "Betreff:", and no comma after "Mit freundlichen Grüßen" are correct.
- US photo: `USPremiumATSTemplate` accepts `photoUrl` but never renders it; only the two German templates render photos, so "per template as today" is accurate.
- `render-caps.ts` is a detector, not the truncation cause; removing it after the `.slice()` calls is fine.
- D1 `batch()` is atomic; the plan's use for refunds is valid.
- Queue/CPU limits: generation is I/O-bound and `cpu_ms: 300000` is enough; the 15-minute consumer wall time fits.
- Browser Run pricing math is right: 10 hours/month included, then $0.09/hour, ~3s/PDF.
- Greenhouse's 100MB upload cap doesn't invalidate the <2MB target; the target is a parse target, only the parenthetical is wrong.
- The 40-tool catalog size is within directory norms; only the count is off by one.
- `requireMcpAuth` already returns 401 with `WWW-Authenticate` and 403 with `insufficient_scope`; the plan doesn't need to hand-roll those.
- `quickAction` can run locally with a remote browser binding; the plan's `wrangler dev --remote` note is a minor tooling detail.

